 \\\"\\\"0.18\\\"\\\": null}\\\",67.45974824755113529742916119346969412637,,1.463662919027984799142739912864859518458,0.6985810847788885419233731512944773857286,,\\r\\nbull / no_new_finance,core,57257f4ba4ff58a25693c7c63363b639ee8057cfa44158f20aa1dffbb624c36b,False,\\\"{\\\"\\\"date\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"2026-04-30\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"stage\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"cycle1 supplier/payroll payment\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"cash\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"0.42874828767123287671232876712328767126\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"cash_floor\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"3\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"unfunded_gap\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"2.57125171232876712328767123287671232874\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"preentry\\\"\\\": true, \\\"\\\"year\\\"\\\": 0}\\\",46.01290873060611747044473634828297992106,137.9915208219178082191780821917808219177,0,0,0,,,\\\"{\\\"\\\"0.12\\\"\\\": null, \\\"\\\"0.15\\\"\\\": null, \\\"\\\"0.18\\\"\\\": null}\\\",133.1670779975511352974291611934696941265,,2.889303905865058395344058320946496023817,1,,\\r\\nbull / bounded_optional,core,13cd592ffba647e9d0b8952c2041182d97b1c239f3ff39bbfab03bec862d5442,True,,48.42664485858510039407018202289360105070,140.5627725342465753424657534246575342464,0,0,2.57125171232876712328767123287671232874,1340.047453723533753098590855799199823615,0.420860250161073317772357784917864787508,\\\"{\\\"\\\"0.12\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"760.3789131800600839914304171859695723816\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"0.15\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"666.2404181870524939263632684829629862125\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"0.18\\\"\\\": \\\"\\\"585.7470922087155102323772638160812392246\\\"\\\"}\\\",126.7590512055990805029086132482642146743,11.24079459409282266559554363654782542076,2.750270016128321158760798665824053102442,1,2.105627234205700166861733056620533031775,5.791043447379143271817592289538460776213\\r\\n\"","\"# RBM Infracon Limited | SME deep research | Master Prompt v3.0\\n\\nInformation cutoff: 20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata. Market observation: NSE close, 19 October 2026. ₹ crore unless specified. This is analytical research, not a trade instruction or an allocation recommendation.\\n\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\nRBM is an industrial EPC, maintenance and turnaround contractor adding oilfield production-enhancement work. Its expansion from ₹83.19 crore revenue in FY23 to ₹492.22 crore in FY26 is commercially significant, but the attractive-looking reported earnings multiple is not enough to make it an investable compounder. The decisive issue is cash and financial reliability: the FY26 auditor qualified ₹107.77 crore of unbilled revenue and could not verify warrant-fund utilization; operating cash flow was negative for four consecutive years; debt increased sharply while supplier, customer and lender financing supported a much larger working-capital base. Management says the unbilled amount was subsequently billed and the proceeds issue addressed, but billing is not collection and the retained statutory opinion remains qualified. The correct classification is Hold — unresolved financial reliability and funding, with BQS, GQS and IAS NS, not a low-ranked fabricated score. This is a potential execution/collection special situation, not an evidence-backed compounder. Auditor-backed reconciliation, actual collections and a current funded liquidity bridge could change that conclusion; a cheaper quotation alone cannot. [E02–E10; A26; R26]\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n- Exact issuer: RBM Infracon Limited, NSE symbol RBMINFRA, active SME series SM, ISIN INE0NA301016. The official 19 October security master reports 10,633,000 issued shares and a 200-share lot. Its inactive SQ record contains an old share count and is not used. The official close is ₹231.40, not the ₹231.05 last transaction. [E01; NSE; MASTER]\\n- Legal identity changed location/CIN over time: the IPO document shows U45400UP2013PLC055914; FY26 shows L42909GJ2013PLC157450. These are the same verified issuer, not RBM Industries or another infrastructure company. The business's predecessor history is older than the 2013 company incorporation; predecessor longevity is not equivalent to an audited listed-entity track record. [IPO; A26]\\n- Scope: FY20–FY25 standalone; FY26 consolidated, with standalone cross-check. FY26 parent and consolidated revenue are identical; consolidated PAT ₹45.2755 crore versus standalone ₹45.2839 crore in revised results; the annual-report standalone P&L instead shows ₹45.2821 crore. That ₹0.0018 crore vintage difference is retained, not silently harmonized. Green Energy is wholly owned and Solar Solutions is 60% owned. No acquired-revenue bridge is assumed. A26 consolidation uses AS 21, and construction accounting references AS 7. These are Accounting Standards (AS) under the Companies (Accounting Standards) Rules, not assumed Ind AS. [E19]\\n- Latest audited balance-sheet date: 31 March 2026. H2 FY26 is a balancing half-year, not a standalone quarter. H1 FY27 results were not available by the cutoff. No annualization of a seasonal half-year or post-cutoff result is used.\\n- Horizon: five years from 19 October 2026, plus a one-year terminal funding test. Equity hurdle: 15% nominal INR before personal tax; sensitivities 12% and 18%. Analytical nominal INR discount range: 12–18%, distinct from the return hurdle. The primary illustrative discount rate is 15%.\\n- Liquidity reference: ₹5 lakh, not a recommended position. Brokerage, taxes and market impact are not reliably quantifiable; a 2% round-trip execution cost would reduce a positive five-year wealth multiple by 2%, and is not embedded as a fictitious exact expense.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason and evidence required to resolve it |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 Integrity/minority protection | Hold — unresolved | The use-of-proceeds verification and control contradictions require reconciliation. No substantiated diversion or severe minority abuse was established; do not convert an audit qualification into a fraud allegation. Auditor-backed funds-use schedule and control remediation required. |\\n| G2 Financial reliability | Hold — unresolved | Material unbilled revenue, opaque advances, conflicting audit/board assertions, historical reclassifications and EPS discrepancies. IAS remains NS; numerical quality rankings are also excluded. Independently verified billing/collections, reconciled statements and auditor closure required. |\\n| G3 Funding/survival | Hold — unresolved | Current cash, drawing power, covenants and post-March debt are unverified. Credible linked stresses require rescue; this is not evidence that an actual default already occurred. Updated lender statements, maturity ladder and collections required. |\\n| G4 Tradability | Conditional | All 60 sessions traded, but spreads, executable depth and current surveillance restrictions remain unverified. Lot and practical participation constrain implementation. |\\n\\nConfidence is High that the specified primary disclosures and exchange records say what is recorded; Medium on business description, concentration history and persistent cash weakness; Low on reliability of current recognized earnings, asset recoverability, incremental returns and investable valuation. Overall conclusion confidence is Low because the weakest material subject is financial reliability. A reproducible model is not proof its economic assumptions are true.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\n| Measure | Known sum | Coverage | Raw | Raw mathematical bound | Cap | Final / capped bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 2.86 | 76.00% | NS | ['3.10', '5.26'] | None | NS / ['3.10', '5.26'] |\\n| GQS | 1.20 | 30.00% | NS | ['1.90', '8.20'] | 5 | NS / ['1.90', '5.00'] |\\n| IAS | 1.00 | 30.00% | NS | ['1.70', '8.00'] | None | NS / ['1.70', '8.00'] |\\n\\nThe NS aggregates are not zero and their missing weights are not redistributed. Bounds are mathematical possibilities, not forecasts or confidence intervals. IAS additionally propagates the narrower BQS bound rather than treating unknown BQS as any number from 1 to 10; its exact propagated interval is 2.225–6.815, retained unrounded in score_audit.json. No midpoint is ranked.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence | Basis and contrary evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | E03, E09, E10, E11, E16 | Recurring statutory delays, historical internal-audit absence and contradictory proceeds-use reporting materially weaken stewardship. Remuneration escalated despite negative CFO. Contrary: No fraud or default was reported. Independent directors, audit-trail/IFC reports and promoter collateral provide contrary support. No substantiated diversion, so no automatic GOV ≤ 3 or G1 failure. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | E12, E13, E14 | Maintenance track record and customer qualification help win repeat work, but competitive tenders and customer/project concentration prevent verified pricing power. Contrary: The multiyear ONGC relationship and execution experience may support entry barriers; excess cash returns and a defined market share are unproven. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | E02, E03, E20 | Reported five- and three-year CAGRs reach the 9 starting anchor, but ₹107.77 crore latest unbilled revenue and changing gross/WIP mix prevent a reliable underlying-growth score. NS is not an arbitrary penalty. Contrary: Even qualification-adjusted revenue implies substantial multiyear expansion. No assumption is made that all growth is fabricated. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | E12, E13, E14, E15 | Recurring maintenance and a long ONGC relationship create accessible demand. Scaling remains constrained by cash, production and concentrated Epitome execution. Contrary: Semiconductor TAM is excluded; established customer relationships and new field work offer genuine opportunities. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | E04, E05, E07, E18 | Large FY26 ONGC and land investments, and ₹110.96 crore other advances, prevent attribution of incremental returns. Aggregate proxies cannot validate cohort economics. Contrary: Reported EBIT/NOPAT growth is positive and some assets are commissioned; this is not proof that capital was destroyed. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Medium | E05, E06, E08 | Four consecutive negative-CFO years, cumulative collection deficits and older receivables despite large customer/supplier funding show persistent poor realization. Contrary: Some absorption funds genuine growth. The conclusion survives all retained CFO vintages and is not merely one year of capex. |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | E07, E06, E18 | Cash of ₹6.58 crore versus debt of ₹142.22 crore, large demand/bill exposure and guarantees imply funding fragility. The model requires rapid collections or optional credit. Contrary: No default was reported and the June 2026 rating improved to BBB+/A2. A rating is not verified drawable headroom. G3 is a hold, not proven actual insolvency. |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium | E12, E13, E03 | Revenue, equipment and workover milestones are real, but Epitome timing and unbilled-certification/mainboard promises remain unresolved. Higher throughput is not equivalent to collection. Contrary: Ten CWP workovers were reported complete. H2 weakness was attributed to supply-chain issues, but independent milestone cash acceptance is missing. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | E02, E03, E04, E10 | Reported PAT is largely operating, yet a ₹13.1163 crore qualification impact and inconsistent EPS prevent reliable normalized per-share growth. Contrary: Other income is only ₹0.5136 crore, dilution is bounded and current issued capital is verified. |\\n| R | NS | Low | E18, E03, E07 | There is no defensible unconditional base IRR because of pre-entry cash failures and unresolved financial reliability. Conditional scenarios are illustrations only. Contrary: The optional-finance bull case works under strong collection and margin assumptions, but is not a committee-ready base case. |\\n| D | 2 | Medium | E03, E05, E07, E18 | A credible permanent-loss path arises from uncertified work, uncollected advances and senior claims. Returns are suppressed after any cash breach. Contrary: Reported net assets and customer support could recover value. NS recovery price is not an assertion of zero recovery. |\\n| P | NS | Low | E04, E18 | Low reported P/E cannot establish implied expectations until earnings and funding are reliable. Reverse trials test conditional required economics. Contrary: The quotation implies less than the strong bull case, but unverified asset quality dominates the multiple. |\\n| L | 6 | Medium | E01, E17 | The 2.43-session exit at 5% participation starts at score 7. An explicit one-point downgrade for unverified spread/depth and SME lot/circuit execution gives 6. Contrary: Every one of 60 sessions traded, and a ₹46,280 lot fits the reference position. A lower price is not evidence of absent liquidity. |\\n\\nCanonical weights: BQS GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%; GQS REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%; IAS BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%. Every multiplication is retained in score_audit.json with Decimal precision40; displays use conventional half-up one-decimal rounding. BQS is reused unrounded in IAS when scoreable. No extra arbitrary risk haircut is applied.\\n\\nCASH=2 reflects persistent poor realization, so the explicit GQS cap is 5, while GQS stays NS. GOV=4 does not trigger the governance cap; G1 is not an evidenced failure. G3 remains a hold, not an evidenced funding failure, so no invented IAS funding cap is applied. G2 alone is sufficient to prevent a favorable investment conclusion. Moving GOV and DUR separately by ±1 is retained: GOV's hypothetical fall to 3 triggers the explicit governance cap but does not turn NS into a point score. The hypothetical GOV = 3 case caps both BQS and IAS at 3, with nested IAS bounds propagated after the BQS cap; all missing aggregates remain NS. This is a sensitivity, not a misconduct finding. REV's reported CAGR starting band would be 9; latest recognition uncertainty prevents assigning that score. Historical 9–10 scores also require a persistent cycle, not a mechanically large CAGR.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nRBM sells engineering, procurement, construction, fabrication, repair, shutdown and maintenance services to process-industry clients. Mechanical/rotary equipment, piping, tanks, structures, electrical/instrumentation and related site services are the operational context. Customers buy safety, qualification, timely mobilization and reliable execution, but still negotiate price and milestone acceptance. The contractor pays employees, subcontractors, materials and machinery providers before final cash collection. Contract progress may become recognized revenue, then certified bills, then collected cash; these stages must not be conflated. [IPO business section; A26 accounting policies; C26]\\n\\nFY26 has a single disclosed reportable segment. There is no verified segmented profitability or capital-return schedule for EPC versus maintenance versus upstream work. Materials consumed ₹119.2243 crore, net WIP credit ₹7.0495 crore and subcontractor charges ₹188.9650 crore illustrate the large bought-in execution component. Employee costs ₹83.9439 crore include ₹3 crore directors' remuneration. Gross profit/value added is ND because direct labor, site, subcontractor and overhead allocations are not reliably separable. The P&L's operating EBITDA is not gross profit. [A26 notes22–28; E02]\\n\\nThe ONGC Nandej production-enhancement contract adds a different economic risk: field production, equipment availability, development spend, oil-price/FX assumptions and revenue-sharing mechanics. The 15-year term, possible five-year extension and headline ₹3,497 crore contract value are not a near-term receivable. Management describes base-production fees and a share of incremental production; the exact monthly-versus-daily minimum-production language in the call is insufficiently consistent to construct a bankable well-level model. No blanket annual revenue division of the headline contract is used. [C26 pp7–9; CRA25 p2]\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\nMechanism: repeated safe site execution and customer qualification can lower a customer's switching appetite. Evidence: continued maintenance relationships, named industrial customers and the ONGC work programme. Durability: useful but dependent on execution, key staff and competitive tendering. Failure mode: delays, accidents, inadequate performance, customer concentration, aggressive bids or inability to fund mobilization. Large clients do not remove certification delays or bargaining power. [E12–E14]\\n\\nMechanism: owning specialized ONGC-related equipment could improve control and reduce hired-equipment expense. Evidence: net ONGC assets of ₹71.0846 crore appear in FY26 PPE. Durability: returns depend on utilization and production, not merely equipment ownership. Failure mode: underperforming wells or imported parts, weak production sharing, large replacement costs or capital tied to one field. No superior unit economics have yet been established. [A26 PPE note12/13; C26]\\n\\nThe 2022 prospectus disclosed 88.06% of FY22 sales from five customers, versus 88.32% in FY21 and 98.70% in FY20; FY26 customer revenue shares are ND. The May 2025 rating rationale attributed 75% of the ₹1,245.85 crore EPC backlog to Epitome and separately described ONGC. Gujarat contributed approximately 88% of FY24 revenue. Do not add these different denominators into a single spurious concentration measure. Supplier concentration was also disclosed, but the prospectus's period ordering is inconsistent; the clear lesson is concentrated sourcing, not a precisely comparable current percentage. [IPO risks; CRA25]\\n\\n| Comparator | Relevant economic comparison | Important difference |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Engineers India | Engineering, refinery/petrochemical EPC and project management; a more established technical/qualification benchmark | Consultancy, design and technology ownership differ from labor/subcontract-heavy site execution. The official profile documents engineering capacity and process technologies; not a direct multiple transfer. |\\n| Deep Industries | Equipment-led oilfield services and a 15-year ONGC production-enhancement contract | Closer upstream capital/production comparator, but charter-hire/service mix and group assets differ. Its FY26 chairman letter confirms active field development, not a guarantee of RBM's economics. |\\n| K2 Infragen | Smaller-company EPC/turnkey, power and project engineering | A process/funding-risk comparator rather than proof of inferior or superior cash quality. No unverified peer score or current valuation multiple is assigned. |\\n\\nSources: PEER_EIL official profile; PEER_DEEP FY26 report p2; PEER_K2 official business description. This peer set frames the economics. A contemporaneous normalized numeric peer valuation is ND; the primary cross-check below instead uses RBM's qualification-adjusted earnings and net assets. No unsupported peer-average fair value is fabricated.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\n| Period | Start sales | End sales | Reported CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23–FY26 | 83.19 | 492.22 | 80.87% |\\n| FY21–FY26 | 38.71 | 492.22 | 66.29% |\\n\\nTen-year CAGR is ND: eleven comparable observations were not located. The complete seven-year reported series and each annual rupee addition appear below. FY21 had a pandemic-affected loss, but its revenue was not a zero denominator. Three-year expansion is independently large; neither the small starting scale nor the pandemic label justifies hiding the absolute additions.\\n\\n| FY | Revenue growth | ₹ addition | EBITDA margin | PAT margin | Average AR days | ROIC proxy | ROCE proxy | ROE | Current-share EPS |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY20 | NS | NS | 2.48% | -0.34% | NS | NS | NS | NS | -0.11 |\\n| FY21 | 10.33% | 3.62 | -1.10% | -3.44% | 146.46 | -7.83% | -7.61% | -26.57% | -1.25 |\\n| FY22 | 22.80% | 8.83 | 7.18% | 4.02% | 119.24 | 16.12% | 20.91% | 34.23% | 1.80 |\\n| FY23 | 74.98% | 35.65 | 5.64% | 2.66% | 74.67 | 14.78% | 19.51% | 18.60% | 2.08 |\\n| FY24 | 55.94% | 46.53 | 14.54% | 8.55% | 54.27 | 22.58% | 28.20% | 19.38% | 10.43 |\\n| FY25 | 148.02% | 192.02 | 13.35% | 9.16% | 49.47 | 25.66% | 32.39% | 24.75% | 27.72 |\\n| FY26 | 52.98% | 170.47 | 14.95% | 9.20% | 66.28 | 21.54% | 27.98% | 27.56% | 42.58 |\\n\\nFY26 consolidated sales rose 53.00%, but H2 sales ₹208.2226 crore fell 4.65% against H2 FY25 ₹218.3751 crore after H1 sales rose strongly. H1 FY26 sales ₹283.9941 crore compares with derived H1 FY25 ₹103.3707 crore. Half-year profit also slowed: H2 ₹18.3615 crore versus ₹19.5677 crore. The two halves should not be interpreted as four comparable quarters. [R26 results]\\n\\nGrowth bridge: new EPC scope, more materials/subcontracted work, a larger operating footprint and the first upstream assets plausibly explain significant expansion. No material acquired-revenue contribution was disclosed in the reviewed group statements, but that does not establish an audited organic/constant-price/volume bridge. Historical subsidiaries and consistent revenue perimeter help comparability; exact price, volume, new-customer and mix contributions are ND. FY26's ₹107.7677 crore unbilled amount equals a material part of annual sales. Deducting it produces alternative revenue of about ₹384.45 crore, not ₹384.96 crore: the latter is total income including other income. This alternative is an audit-impact diagnostic, not a revised clean revenue series. [E04; E20]\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nDemand access is credible in maintenance, turnaround and process-industry projects, while upstream production adds duration. Accessible growth is constrained by execution cash, not by a broad national infrastructure TAM. There is no verified company market share or defined addressable-market denominator, so implied market share remains ND rather than being inferred from a promotional national spending figure.\\n\\nThe modeled five-year revenue paths are cash-linked execution envelopes, not backlog multiplication: bear ₹380/390/410/440/470 crore; base ₹550/630/710/790/860 crore; bull ₹650/780/920/1,060/1,200 crore. Base FY1 is below management's ₹700 crore FY27 target and recognizes slower collection/execution. Each path funds receivables, new unbilled work, WIP, supplier advances, retention and fixed assets, while customer advances and payables provide explicit, bounded operating support. A ₹100 crore increment at base assumptions requires operating receivables of ₹26.03 crore, WIP ₹15.5 crore, supplier advances ₹14 crore, new unbilled ₹6.85 crore, retention ₹0.7 crore and deposits ₹1 crore, offset by payables ₹25 crore, customer advances ₹22 crore and accruals ₹2.4 crore. Incremental fixed capital is another ₹23 crore before maintenance. These are assumptions, not disclosed contract terms. [model_inputs.json]\\n\\nThis shows why growth and free cash are not interchangeable. Full reinvestment accompanies each model increase, not merely an EPS uplift. The simple growth ≈ reinvestment-rate × incremental-return identity cannot validate the forecast because incremental return is NS. The model nevertheless rejects terminal valuation where replacement/growth investment and cash survival cannot coexist.\\n\\nFY26 PPE additions include land ₹30.8105 crore and ONGC gross additions ₹74.406 crore, alongside other equipment. The ₹13.6565 crore prior development/CWIP amount transfers through the PPE schedule; adding it to cash capex again would double count. Net PPE/intangibles ₹113.154 crore is not the same as gross block. The call's shorthand “gross block ₹113.11 crore” matches net tangible PPE rather than the full gross schedule. [A26 PPE schedule; C26]\\n\\nSemiconductor optionality is excluded from base operating revenue and valuation. April's LOI/MoU requires diligence, approvals and definitive agreements; a stated FDI commitment is not proof cash is available to RBM's existing creditors. Its 30% RBM/70% partner proposed equity conflicts with some June-call phrasing about promoters holding 70%; an initial ₹60 crore land/infrastructure discussion conflicts with deferring contribution for three to four years. The sensitivity therefore tests actual ₹60 crore and ₹300 crore outflows without giving credit for speculative semiconductor proceeds. A separate definitive funding model would be required before treating this as value. [MOU; C26; E15]\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\nThe promoter family owns approximately 62.50% at March 2026. The FY24 preferential issue added 1,662,000 shares at ₹386, gross ₹64.1532 crore; 530,000 promoter warrants at the same price had total consideration ₹20.458 crore and converted on 12 August 2025. ₹1.1535 crore of the warrant money came in FY26; do not add the entire conversion price as new cash again. Current shares are 25.97% above FY23's 8.441 million, excluding the earlier bonus effect. FY24 warrants/reserves were subsequently reclassified without changing total equity. The original IPO was 2,325,000 fresh shares for ₹8.37 crore. [IPO; A24 CARO; A25 warrant note; A26 share capital]\\n\\nWarrant utilization remains a verification issue, not proof of theft: the FY26 main opinion says supporting information was not furnished, while CARO10(b) says the proceeds were used for the intended purpose. The board's listing/trading-approval explanation establishes admission of shares, not how money was spent. June's statement that a utilization certificate was filed and September's “subsequently billed” assertion are management claims; neither independently removes the signed audit qualification. The board describes the unbilled amount as relating to September 2024, whereas the FY26 auditor refers to the year ended March 2026 and the impact form says half-year. Preserve this period conflict. [A26 pp23,45,52,86; R26; C26]\\n\\nFY26 directors' remuneration of ₹3 crore versus ₹0.7325 crore in the comparative payroll line is a substantial increase. A spouse received ₹0.6 crore salary, and family rents total about ₹0.3906 crore. Directors' remuneration plus spouse salary is about 7.95% of reported consolidated PAT and much more material against negative CFO. The notes also show ₹5.597 crore interest-free promoter loans without specific terms, and promoter/relative collateral backing facilities. That support is economically useful, but it is repayable funding and potential concentration, not free equity. FY25 related-party notes show promoter loans given/recovered; the bridge to all subsequent balances and terms needs fuller explanation. [A26 related-party notes]\\n\\nThe IPO restatement also has material reliability limitations: F9 describes gratuity and leave on a pay-as-you-go basis and says profit is overstated by an unquantified amount; F10 makes loans, creditors, debtors and advances subject to confirmation, with effects unascertainable. Restatement auditor Raichura relied on predecessor SSRS/Sarvesh audits rather than routinely re-auditing them. The statement that no qualifications required restatement adjustment is not blanket proof of clean books. [IPO F9–F10]\\n\\nAuditor/control history is not summarized as “clean”: FY23 and FY24 main opinions and IFC opinions were unmodified, but FY24 CARO said a required internal-audit system was absent, described no inventory despite significant WIP, and reported bank-return reconciliation differences. FY25 had joint MB Jajodia/Patel Jain signers, a prior statutory-auditor resignation noted in CARO, overdue income-tax/TDS and recurring bank-return differences. FY26 continued statutory-payment irregularity but reported none over six months and no loan default; unmodified IFC is not an assurance on recoverability of each balance. The FY26 impact form's “second time” qualification does not override FY25's actual unmodified main opinion. [A23–A26 full audit/CARO/IFC]\\n\\n| Dated commitment / milestone | Evidence by cutoff | Assessment |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Epitome project funded/on schedule in March 2024 and May 2025 rationales | June 2026 call discusses delays, large machinery advances and later completion timing | Order value is not executed cash; full original/updated schedule and certified work ledger needed. |\\n| ONGC CWP, February–May 2025 | July 2025 issuer release reports ten workovers completed within timetable | Positive execution milestone, management reported; not a cash-collection certificate. |\\n| June 2026: unbilled certification expected by June; warrant certificate said filed | September annual report still contains signed qualified opinions; board says billed | Unresolved independent closure. |\\n| June 2026: mainboard migration in about 20–25 days | Official 19 October master remains SM with 200-share lot | Promise not verified as delivered; no invented migration date. |\\n| June 2026: no parent dilution next six to eight months | Observed October issued shares remain 10.633 million | Consistent to cutoff; does not preapprove or fund later equity needs. |\\n| April semiconductor definitive agreements within 90 days | June still early-stage discussion; no verified fully funded close by cutoff | Do not treat proposed project as operating asset or committed rescue. |\\n\\nThe September CFO resignation cites other professional commitments and says no other material reason; an interim CFO was appointed. Internal-auditor changes in July/August are another continuity item. No adverse resignation motive is invented. [CFO; E16]\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\n| FY | Revenue | Operating EBITDA | EBIT | PAT | Normalized PAT proxy | CFO | Cash capex | FCF |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY20 | 35.09 | 0.87 | 0.43 | -0.12 | -0.14 | 0.84 | 2.11 | -1.27 |\\n| FY21 | 38.71 | -0.43 | -0.94 | -1.33 | -1.54 | -0.36 | 0.58 | -0.94 |\\n| FY22 | 47.54 | 3.41 | 2.91 | 1.91 | 1.78 | 0.83 | 1.02 | -0.19 |\\n| FY23 | 83.19 | 4.69 | 3.94 | 2.21 | 2.26 | -4.31 | 2.08 | -6.38 |\\n| FY24 | 129.73 | 18.86 | 17.59 | 11.09 | 11.80 | -51.74 | 2.57 | -54.31 |\\n| FY25 | 321.75 | 42.95 | 40.77 | 29.47 | 29.46 | -12.16 | 21.28 | -33.44 |\\n| FY26 | 492.22 | 73.57 | 67.46 | 45.28 | 46.09 | -25.58 | 94.82 | -120.40 |\\n\\nReported numbers are retained, not silently replaced by adjusted estimates. Normalized PAT proxy removes all other income and applies an analytical 25% tax to positive PBT; it does not resolve unbilled recognition, provisions or owner attribution. Therefore normalized EARN remains NS. Current-share EPS rebases group PAT to today's 10.633 million shares for comparison, not historical statutory EPS. The original diluted EPS, current-share EPS and inconsistent FY26 filing EPS are separate fields. No non-recurring profits were fabricated to improve the normalization.\\n\\n| Window | CFO | PAT | CFO/PAT | FCF | Cash after capex and finance |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | -89.48 | 85.84 | -1.04 | -208.15 | -217.51 |\\n| 4y | -93.78 | 88.05 | -1.07 | -214.53 | -224.81 |\\n| 5y | -92.95 | 89.96 | -1.03 | -214.72 | -225.54 |\\n\\nCumulative CFO/PAT is sum(CFO)/sum(PAT), never an average of annual ratios. All reported vintages remain weak: FY23 CFO changes from −₹3.9783 to −₹4.3059 crore; FY24 from −₹50.4387 to −₹51.7387 crore; FY25 from −₹3.4409 to −₹12.1637 crore. The FY24 change reallocates ₹1.3 crore between investments and advances. FY25 reallocates about ₹8.7227 crore of FD release into investing and also changes deposit, advance and gratuity presentation. Cash totals are unaffected apart from disclosed rounding. The original FY25 cash components call ₹5.6622 crore a fixed account, while the next comparative presents substantially the same amount as current-bank cash. The model therefore excludes separate long-maturity FD ₹11.8231 crore and tests making most nominal cash unavailable. [E08; retained vintage ledger]\\n\\nFY26's cash bridge is especially instructive: ₹74.2013 crore operating cash before working-capital changes was outweighed by ₹44.2261 crore receivables growth, ₹7.0495 crore WIP and ₹229.5558 crore advances. Trade-payable growth ₹114.7399 crore and other current liabilities ₹72.2126 crore partly funded that absorption. Other noncurrent assets, tax-provision movement, gratuity payment and cash tax complete reported CFO −₹25.5758 crore. Then ₹94.824 crore cash capex, ₹5.1165 crore further FD investment and interest drain require new debt. Gross new borrowings ₹131.375 crore, ₹1.1575 crore equity/minority receipts and other items left only ₹0.8741 crore net cash increase. Every term is retained; CFO is not charged working capital twice. Interest ₹6.0119 crore is financing in the cash-flow statement, so the after-finance FCF proxy deducts it separately. [A26 consolidated cash flow]\\n\\nCollection quality: billed receivables ₹111.4963 crore include ₹24.3173 crore more than a year past due, versus ₹2.0835 crore a year earlier. Balances older than six months are ₹26.8581 crore, with no doubtful-debt provision. The notes mark amounts good based on management certification. Separate unbilled ₹107.7678 crore and retention ₹3.4157 crore make the billed-only debtor-day ratio incomplete; at year-end, billed plus unbilled plus retention is roughly 165 days of reported sales. WIP ₹76.1745 crore and supplier advances ₹68.8794 crore add execution exposure. “Other loans and advances” ₹110.9565 crore require counterparties, ageing, security and recoverability evidence before being treated as liquid or productive operating capital. [A26 notes18–21; E05–E06]\\n\\nFunding: gross debt ₹142.2245 crore includes ₹59.9415 crore noncurrent debt and ₹82.283 crore short-term debt/current maturities. The latter includes bank/NBFC cash credit, ₹33.4495 crore vendor-bill discounting, ₹10 crore intercorporate borrowing and ₹7.6487 crore current term maturities. Bill discounting is financing, not cash conversion. The apparent net-debt/EBITDA ratio around 1.84x and EBIT/finance-cost around 11.22x look comfortable only if qualified earnings are reliable and year-average interest approximates the much larger closing debt; neither is enough to establish resilience. Rates include 9–10% secured facilities and 14–16% unsecured business loans. [A26 debt notes]\\n\\nFD collateral requirements of 20%/30%, hypothecated assets and an ONGC receivables escrow matter to fungibility. ₹8.8925 crore bank guarantees, ₹0.9478 crore disputed income-tax claims and other statutory disputes/dues are not ignored. The June 2026 rating upgrade to BBB+/Stable and A2 on ₹106.90 crore facilities is supportive but does not certify incremental drawable availability, nor reconcile every borrowing in the March balance sheet. The latest complete rationale located was May 2025; its historically adequate liquidity and 87% cash-credit utilization should not be carried forward over a twelvefold increase in debt. May 2025’s rationale table gives FY24 debt as ₹10.60 crore, versus ₹2.1911 crore in the audited FY24/FY25 statements; absent a reconciliation, the model uses audited debt, not the rating table. A non-cooperation footnote in that document does not override its explicit “Nil” status and upgrade, so no non-cooperation allegation is made. [CRA25; CRA26; A26]\\n\\n| Window | Δ NOPAT | Δ financing IC | Incremental proxy | Δ PPE | Δ other IC |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1y | 20.02 | 176.65 | 11.33% | 88.71 | 87.94 |\\n| 3y | 47.64 | 298.12 | 15.98% | 109.12 | 189.00 |\\n| 5y | 51.54 | 311.06 | 16.57% | 110.96 | 200.11 |\\n\\nBroad operating IC ₹285.4526 crore versus financing IC ₹323.2749 crore, residual ₹37.8223 crore. Detailed component inputs are retained in historical_inputs.json; tax/GST, restricted FDs and other liabilities explain the definition gap, not a hidden return on new plant.\\n\\nROIC uses operating EBIT after analytical tax over average financing-based IC; ROCE uses EBIT over average equity-plus-debt. These are diagnostic proxies with an explicit perimeter, not interchangeable metrics. Financing IC includes warrant funds as capital already supplied; restricted cash and tax balances create definition differences. The large FY26 investment/advances and uncertain cost/revenue matching mean the observed incremental proxy cannot identify the return on ONGC assets, Epitome or new land. A reasonable nominal INR cost-of-capital range of 12–18% is an assumption; CAP is NS rather than falsely concluding that a noisy proxy proves excess returns. Maintenance capex is tested at 1.5%, 2.5% and 4% of sales; depreciation is not automatically maintenance spending.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n₹231.40 × 10,633,000 shares = ₹246.04762 crore. Reported P/E 5.43x; qualification-adjusted P/E 7.65x; P/B 1.31x. March-anchored EV ₹381.70 crore and EV/operating EBITDA 5.19x are not verified current enterprise value. 60-session mean/median traded value ₹6558712.83/₹4108965.00; 20-session mean/median ₹4053240.00/₹3545500.00. At 5% participation, ₹5 lakh needs 2.4337 sessions; at 2%, 6.0843, and at 10%, 1.2169. All 60 sessions traded. One 200-share lot costs ₹46,280; 10 lots ₹462,800; 11 lots ₹509,080.\\n\\nThe qualification-adjusted P/E and book value are cross-checks, not investable floors. The impact form's adjusted group PAT ₹32.1592 crore is about ₹13.1163 crore below reported PAT; it includes cost and tax matching, so subtracting the entire unbilled revenue from PAT would be wrong. Its adjusted net assets are ₹174.5125 crore, but the large uncertain advances and senior claims mean no credible liquidation value per share is established. There is no fair-value point estimate or unconditional buy-below price.\\n\\nThe core cash-linked paths are:\\n\\n| Case | First breach | Entry cash | New shares | Terminal price | 5Y return |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear / no_new_finance | 2026-04-30 cycle1 supplier/payroll payment | -175.07 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| bear / bounded_optional | 2026-04-30 cycle4 supplier/payroll payment | -156.29 | 5762028 | NS | NS |\\n| base / no_new_finance | 2026-04-30 cycle1 supplier/payroll payment | -42.69 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| base / bounded_optional | 2026-06-30 cycle1 supplier/payroll payment | -23.76 | 4587853 | NS | NS |\\n| bull / no_new_finance | 2026-04-30 cycle1 supplier/payroll payment | 46.01 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| bull / bounded_optional | None | 48.43 | 0 | 1340.05 | 42.09% |\\n\\n“Entry cash” is an assumed reconstruction from the last audited March balance sheet, not actual October cash. A negative diagnostic bridge means those assumptions cannot describe a funded going concern without additional evidence or finance; it does not assert the issuer actually ran out of cash on that historical date. Apart from the explicit opening cash-availability event on 31 March 2026, each event date is the monthly accounting endpoint, with ordered within-period payments/receipts, not a known bank-statement transaction date. New emergency funding accrues interest from the next period, reflecting assumed month-end draws; this is a limitation, not a claim that intramonth borrowing is free or conservative.\\n\\nModel mechanics are fully reconstructible:\\n\\n1. March cash ₹6.5784 crore, debt ₹142.2245 crore and operating balances initialize seven pre-entry periods through 19 October. Prospective entry cash is never reset to March cash. Pre-entry equity is prohibited because October's share count is verified.\\n2. Monthly modeled suppliers/payroll are paid before receipts in four settlement cycles; one/two/eight cycles are stressed. Cash taxes, capex, interest, term repayments, statutory settlement and optional guarantee calls have separate cash events. First breach and every later diagnostic event remain in the ledger.\\n3. No-new-finance paths require ₹74.6343 crore demand/bill/intercorporate debt to settle at the analytical one-year boundary. Actual on-demand call timing may be earlier. Bounded-optional paths assume renewal instead, not an imaginary new cash inflow. Existing revolver rollover remains conditional throughout the forecast and terminal year; the additional optional bank balance must be repaid in the terminal-year cash test. Incremental bank funding is capped at ₹20 crore, a hypothetical limit, not approved headroom inferred from the credit rating.\\n4. After entry only, equity may cover a cash shortfall at ₹173.55 per share, a 25% issue discount, 3% fees, ₹100 crore gross cap and authorized-share headroom. New shares round UP to whole shares needed but remain within the gross cap. Receipts and fees immediately affect cash; shares immediately affect dilution. Failed pre-entry paths never become ordinary return paths merely because a later rescue occurs.\\n5. Base assumptions collect 50% of old unbilled balances over three years and 25% of other advances over two years; bull uses 100% and 75%, bear zero. Normal new unbilled work is separately funded. Remaining old assets are not added as free terminal cash. No FD release, tax refund or semiconductor funding is invented.\\n6. Terminal value is earned FCFF6/(discount rate−growth), after replacement capex, growth fixed capital and operating working capital. Senior debt, NCI, required cash and any tax-reserve sensitivity are deducted once. A further year tests payment ordering, maturities and no additional financing. Failed cash/terminal tests suppress ordinary terminal price and IRR rather than show a meaningless EPS-based return.\\n\\nThe only core path that can produce a conditional ordinary return relies on strong collections and bull economics. It is not a supported base case. Its high numerical price is a warning about sensitivity, not a recommendation: the retained cash and low terminal capital intensity must actually be earned, while the model has no verified order-level margins or advances recovery. Output also decomposes price change into earnings growth × share dilution × implied terminal multiple; the multiple is derived from cash capitalization, not separately assumed rerating. No dividends are added to retained terminal cash.\\n\\nTwo-way operating sensitivity, keeping the bull execution/collection bridge and varying post-entry EBIT margin and billed-receivable days:\\n\\n| EBIT margin / DSO | Cash survives | Price | Return |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| margin=.06 / DSO=60 | True | 469.65 | 15.21% |\\n| margin=.06 / DSO=75 | True | 411.33 | 12.19% |\\n| margin=.06 / DSO=95 | True | 333.37 | 7.58% |\\n| margin=.06 / DSO=130 | True | 182.96 | -4.59% |\\n| margin=.08 / DSO=60 | True | 680.02 | 24.06% |\\n| margin=.08 / DSO=75 | True | 621.70 | 21.85% |\\n| margin=.08 / DSO=95 | True | 543.73 | 18.63% |\\n| margin=.08 / DSO=130 | True | 367.46 | 9.69% |\\n| margin=.10 / DSO=60 | True | 890.39 | 30.93% |\\n| margin=.10 / DSO=75 | True | 832.06 | 29.17% |\\n| margin=.10 / DSO=95 | True | 754.10 | 26.65% |\\n| margin=.10 / DSO=130 | True | 558.85 | 19.28% |\\n| margin=.12 / DSO=60 | True | 1100.75 | 36.60% |\\n| margin=.12 / DSO=75 | True | 1042.43 | 35.13% |\\n| margin=.12 / DSO=95 | True | 964.47 | 33.04% |\\n| margin=.12 / DSO=130 | True | 758.61 | 26.80% |\\n| margin=.14 / DSO=60 | True | 1311.12 | 41.47% |\\n| margin=.14 / DSO=75 | True | 1252.80 | 40.19% |\\n| margin=.14 / DSO=95 | True | 1174.83 | 38.40% |\\n| margin=.14 / DSO=130 | True | 967.32 | 33.12% |\\n| margin=.16 / DSO=60 | True | 1521.48 | 45.74% |\\n| margin=.16 / DSO=75 | True | 1463.16 | 44.61% |\\n| margin=.16 / DSO=95 | True | 1385.20 | 43.03% |\\n| margin=.16 / DSO=130 | True | 1176.27 | 38.43% |\\n\\nDiscount-rate/terminal-growth sensitivity, with explicit ongoing reinvestment:\\n\\n| Discount / terminal growth | Survives | Price | Return |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| discount=.12 / terminalg=0 | True | 1357.92 | 42.46% |\\n| discount=.12 / terminalg=.02 | True | 1546.20 | 46.21% |\\n| discount=.12 / terminalg=.03 | True | 1671.09 | 48.50% |\\n| discount=.12 / terminalg=.04 | True | 1826.74 | 51.17% |\\n| discount=.15 / terminalg=0 | True | 1155.72 | 37.94% |\\n| discount=.15 / terminalg=.02 | True | 1269.44 | 40.56% |\\n| discount=.15 / terminalg=.03 | True | 1340.05 | 42.09% |\\n| discount=.15 / terminalg=.04 | True | 1423.15 | 43.81% |\\n| discount=.18 / terminalg=0 | True | 1020.92 | 34.56% |\\n| discount=.18 / terminalg=.02 | True | 1096.47 | 36.50% |\\n| discount=.18 / terminalg=.03 | True | 1141.42 | 37.60% |\\n| discount=.18 / terminalg=.04 | True | 1192.53 | 38.81% |\\n\\nOther sensitivity rows independently vary cash availability, pre-entry capex, financing cap and price, statutory/tax reserve, maintenance spending, interest, guarantee call, old advances and semiconductor cash outflow. Terminal examples that fail a cash test remain NS, not zero recovery. The accessible minimum-input package reconstructs all 247 unique full paths and 371 trial/label references, including failed root attempts; identical candidates are deduplicated by complete effective assumptions without dropping any reference.\\n\\nReverse margin requirements:\\n\\n| Target | Status | Required EBIT margin bracket | Target terminal price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| hurdle12 | cash-feasible margin root | 5.97%–5.97% | 407.81 |\\n| hurdle15 | cash-feasible margin root | 6.51%–6.51% | 465.43 |\\n| hurdle18 | cash-feasible margin root | 7.12%–7.12% | 529.39 |\\n| fivefold | cash-feasible margin root | 13.09%–13.09% | 1157.00 |\\n| tenfold | cash-feasible margin root | 24.09%–24.09% | 2314.00 |\\n\\nEach root uses the explicitly conditional bull revenue/collection path and bounds EBIT margin from 4% to 30%. It is a joint cash-feasibility and price test: a threshold where funding suddenly becomes feasible is not mislabeled a smooth valuation root. Bisection residuals, endpoint paths and every failed trial are saved. Uniform revenue-growth probes at 0/10/20/30/40/50% also rerun all capital and funding links. A root's existence is not evidence that management can deliver it.\\n\\nFor every surviving path, entry limits at 12%, 15% and 18% hurdles are terminal price divided by (1+hurdle)^5. For breached paths they are NS. There is no defensible unconditional entry-price range while G2 and G3 are unresolved. A 15% equity hurdle is not silently substituted for all discount-rate sensitivities.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nThe thesis-break variable is conversion of recognized project progress and advances into collectible cash. A credible linked loss path is: Epitome or upstream execution disappoints → certification slows and old advances fail to unwind → lower margins and larger WIP/unbilled/receivables → customer advances and supplier credit stop expanding → demand/bill lenders require repayment → discounted equity or asset sales become necessary. If rescue cannot be funded, ordinary shareholders are residual claimants after creditors and contractual obligations. A nominally profitable P&L can coexist with permanent capital impairment.\\n\\nThe strongest evidence against a uniformly pessimistic view is real operating scale growth, repeated industrial customers, tangible ONGC investment, reported workover milestones, no disclosed loan default and improved credit ratings. These facts justify research rather than a proven-fraud verdict. They do not answer why four years of profits did not translate into CFO, why large advances lack transparent terms or why the statutory qualification remains. [E02–E14]\\n\\nImpairment risk: High. The model quantifies funding failure, not a fictitious precise liquidation price. Bear-case quote downside is NS because cash survival fails and recovery cannot be established; this is more informative than treating a negative cash balance plus a generous P/E as a valid share price. Even a surviving optional case can suffer dilution, and shareholder return must use its enlarged share count. Mainboard migration or a new contract announcement alone would not repair this failure path.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\nFivefold price appreciation over five years requires 37.97% annualized price growth; tenfold requires 58.49%. At ₹231.40, terminal prices would need to be ₹1,157 and ₹2,314 respectively, before costs and personal tax. The reverse tests solve these targets with the same cash, reinvestment and bounded-financing model rather than multiplying headline orders by a P/E. A large terminal result can arise under the bull case, but would require simultaneous collections, margin, utilization and funding assumptions that are not verified.\\n\\nClassification: Speculative / unsupported, not “10× potential” as a forecast. A large order value spread over fifteen years is not equivalent to fivefold annual revenue. Semiconductor proposed project value is not parent equity value. No precise success probability or synthetic blended IRR is provided.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\nResearch checkpoints only; no recurring automation is created.\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Warning / thesis-break distinction | Review evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Audit closure and unbilled collections | ₹107.7677 crore qualified; board says billed | Warning: billing without receipts. Thesis break: material reversal or independently confirmed non-recoverability | Next results, auditor certificate and customer/bank reconciliation |\\n| Other advances | ₹110.9565 crore, terms/counterparties not established | Warning: balances grow without schedules. Thesis break: unsupported third-party or related-party transfer/impairment | Note-level counterparties, ageing, security and cash recovery |\\n| CFO | FY26 −₹25.5758 crore; four negative years | Warning: further deficit despite collection promises. Break: credible obligations cannot be funded | Comparable cash-flow statements and bank bridge |\\n| Aged trade debtors | >1 year ₹24.3173 crore, nil provision | Warning: roll-forward into older buckets. Break: material loss with inadequate capital | Ageing and subsequent receipts |\\n| Demand/bill debt and liquidity | Debt ₹142.2245 crore; reported cash ₹6.5784 crore | Warning: utilization near limit or rising expensive debt. Break: lender call/default without committed coverage | Sanctions, drawing power, escrow/collateral and maturities |\\n| Epitome execution | Highly concentrated historical backlog, machinery advances | Warning: later certification/completion. Break: cancelled or uneconomic scope with trapped capital | Work certificates, variations, payments and updated schedule |\\n| ONGC economics | Net ONGC assets ₹71.0846 crore; long-duration PEC | Warning: production or equipment availability below plan. Break: sustainable cash returns below debt and replacement needs | Field production, fee/share receipts and segment capital ledger |\\n| Semiconductor commitments | Conditional LOI/MoU, no fully funded close verified | Warning: cash spend before finance close. Break: parent commits obligations it cannot fund | Definitive agreements, ownership and financing proof |\\n| Governance/control remediation | Recurrent bank-return/statutory issues; FY26 qualification | Warning: unresolved contradictions or late filing. Break: evidenced material diversion/minority abuse | Auditor/CARO, internal audit, RPT and compliance records |\\n| Tradability and dilution | SM, 200-share lot, 10.633 million shares, 62.5% promoter ownership at March | Warning: one-sided trades, new warrants or unsupported migration claim | Current NSE master, shareholding, order book and issue terms |\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\nHold — unresolved. Research priority is collection, accounting and funding verification, not promotional upside modeling. BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS with explicit coverage and bounds; no quality, growth or investment point-ranking is permitted. It fails the evidence-backed-compounder criteria because necessary factor and gate conditions are unfulfilled. This does not assert the business has no value or that all reported work is fictitious.\\n\\nThe useful next evidence is narrow: reconcile unbilled amount and corresponding costs by contract/date; document conversion into accepted invoices and bank receipts; explain ₹110.96 crore other advances; obtain a current debt/drawability schedule; reconcile all historical cash-flow and EPS changes; and verify warrant use with auditor support. Only then should a supported base-case IRR and IAS be reconsidered. For an existing holder, this is a reliability/funding/liquidity review candidate, not an automatic sell instruction without position size, tax context and executable market depth.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\n| ID | Document | Reviewed sections |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| A26 | [Annual report FY2026](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31867_RBMINFRA_2025_2026_A_3419639_08092026161118.pdf) (2026-09-08) | PDF pp23,45–55,56–83,86–123: board, full statutory audits/CARO/IFC, statements, accounting policies and notes |\\n| A25 | [Annual report FY2025](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_27740_RBMINFRA_2024_2025_A_17662749_26082025191926.pdf) (2025-08-26) | PDF pp35–44 full audit/CARO/IFC; pp45–75 statements/notes; board and secretarial annexures |\\n| A24 | [Annual report FY2024](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_25857_RBMINFRA_2023_2024_05092024175113.pdf) (2024-09-05) | Full statutory audit/CARO/IFC and financial statements; printed pp33–40 and43–73 |\\n| A23 | [Annual report FY2023](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_23313_RBMINFRA_2022_2023_02092023210105_09022023220003.zip) (2023-09-02) | Full audit/CARO/IFC and printed pp47–76 financials/notes |\\n| IPO | [IPO prospectus](https://rbminfracon.co.in/assets/img/07C.%20PROSPECTUS_RBM.pdf) (2022-12-13) | Risk factors printed pp21–22; business; restated financials F1–F34 including restatement reconciliation; PDF pp154–187 |\\n| R26 | [Revised FY2026 results and audit-qualification impact](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_26062026173117_Revised_Outcome_Signed.pdf) (2026-06-26) | Cover correction acknowledgement; full standalone/consolidated audit; impact statements PDF pp7–8 and19–20; half-year results |\\n| C26 | [FY2026 earnings-call transcript](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_19062026174620_CL_Transcript_Signed.pdf) (2026-06-19) | All 19 pages; particularly pp3–8 unbilled, advances, funding, ONGC structure and semiconductor claims |\\n| CRA24 | [Infomerics credit rationale](https://www.infomerics.com/admin/uploads/PR-RBM-Infracon-14032024.pdf) (2024-03-14) | All 7 pages including facility/covenant annexures; web-read official PDF; local download403 |\\n| CRA25 | [Infomerics credit rationale](https://www.infomerics.com/admin/uploads/Pr-RBM-Infra-13may25.pdf) (2025-05-13) | All 7 pages; backlog/concentration/liquidity and facilities |\\n| CRA26 | [Rating upgrade issuer disclosure](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_25062026193112_Disclosure_of_Rating_Signed.pdf) (2026-06-25) | One-page upgrade BBB+/Stable,A2 on ₹106.90 crore; not fresh lending commitment |\\n| MOU | [Semiconductor LOI/MOU disclosure](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_18042026183252_MOU_Signed.pdf) (2026-04-18) | All 4 pages; conditional definitive agreements/diligence within 90 days |\\n| CFO | [CFO resignation and interim appointment](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_25092026185505_Outcome_of_BM__Resignation_Letter_Signed.pdf) (2026-09-25) | All 5 pages incl resignation reason and new CFO profile |\\n| CWP | [ONGC work-programme completion](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_03072025163211_press_release_Signed.pdf) (2025-07-03) | Management-reported10 workovers February 13–May 13, 2025; not independent cash certification |\\n| NSE | [Official NSE daily bhavcopies](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip) (2026-10-19) | 60 dated sessions, issuer rows only, matched exact ISIN across active series |\\n| MASTER | [Official NSE security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz) (2026-10-19) | Exact-ISIN security rows; SM active SM, 200-share lot, 10,633,000 shares |\\n| PEER_EIL | [Engineers India company profile](https://engineersindia.com/Profile) (Profile, accessed for cutoff research) | Consultancy/EPC scope and technology capabilities; qualitative comparator not scored |\\n| PEER_DEEP | [Deep Industries annual reports](https://www.deepindustries.com/docs/Deep_AR_25-26_Final.pdf) (FY2025–2026 report, available before cutoff) | FY26 chairman letter, PDF p2: charter-hire equipment and 15-year ONGC PEC; qualitative comparator. |\\n| PEER_K2 | [K2 Infragen business description](https://k2infra.com/) (Website, accessed for cutoff research) | EPC/turnkey,power/project engineering; qualitative smaller-EPC comparator |\\n\\nThe evidence ledger distinguishes verified disclosures (V), calculations (C), management claims (M) and analyst assumptions (A). evidence_ledger.json maps every material analytical topic to its source and limits. Original PDF/OCR files, account identifiers, transport helpers and private storage links are excluded from the source-safe deliverables. Source references use public issuer/exchange/rating links. Public filings' exact figures are preserved as factual inputs; long reproduced passages are not included.\\n\\nOpen issues affecting scoreability: independent audit closure, present available cash and credit, other-advance counterparties, bill/retention terms, quantified current customer concentration, backlog roll-forward and cancellation/escalation/liquidated-damage terms, project-level capital returns, actual weighted-average EPS reconciliation, restricted-cash vintage changes and semiconductor definitive financing. A source gap is ND/NS, not an invented neutral score.\\n\\nCalculation definitions and implementation: operating EBITDA=PBT+finance+D&A−other income; EBIT=EBITDA−D&A; NOPAT=EBIT×75% for positive EBIT (no artificial tax benefit on losses); financing IC=equity including paid warrant funds+interest-bearing debt−reported cash, with operating reconciliation separately shown; total-cash-capex FCF=CFO−cash operating capex; interest remains separate where classified in financing. Sales CAGR uses actual year intervals. All equations, input vintages, full event ledgers, factor contributions, reverse candidates and tests can be regenerated using standard-library Python only.\\n\\nUpdate log: initial v3.0 deep-research record for the 20 October cutoff. Material negative audit and cash findings override the apparent low P/E. Retrieval after the cutoff is restricted to documents dated by the cutoff. No unpublished H1 FY27 result, later market quote or hindsight outcome is used.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\nRBM is an industrial EPC, maintenance and turnaround contractor adding oilfield production-enhancement work. Its expansion from ₹83.19 crore revenue in FY23 to ₹492.22 crore in FY26 is commercially significant, but the attractive-looking reported earnings multiple is not enough to make it an investable compounder. The decisive issue is cash and financial reliability: the FY26 auditor qualified ₹107.77 crore of unbilled revenue and could not verify warrant-fund utilization; operating cash flow was negative for four consecutive years; debt increased sharply while supplier, customer and lender financing supported a much larger working-capital base. Management says the unbilled amount was subsequently billed and the proceeds issue addressed, but billing is not collection and the retained statutory opinion remains qualified. The correct classification is Hold — unresolved financial reliability and funding, with BQS, GQS and IAS NS, not a low-ranked fabricated score. This is a potential execution/collection special situation, not an evidence-backed compounder. Auditor-backed reconciliation, actual collections and a current funded liquidity bridge could change that conclusion; a cheaper quotation alone cannot. [E02–E10; A26; R26]\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\n- Exact issuer: RBM Infracon Limited, NSE symbol RBMINFRA, active SME series SM, ISIN INE0NA301016. The official 19 October security master reports 10,633,000 issued shares and a 200-share lot. Its inactive SQ record contains an old share count and is not used. The official close is ₹231.40, not the ₹231.05 last transaction. [E01; NSE; MASTER]\\n- Legal identity changed location/CIN over time: the IPO document shows U45400UP2013PLC055914; FY26 shows L42909GJ2013PLC157450. These are the same verified issuer, not RBM Industries or another infrastructure company. The business's predecessor history is older than the 2013 company incorporation; predecessor longevity is not equivalent to an audited listed-entity track record. [IPO; A26]\\n- Scope: FY20–FY25 standalone; FY26 consolidated, with standalone cross-check. FY26 parent and consolidated revenue are identical; consolidated PAT ₹45.2755 crore versus standalone ₹45.2839 crore in revised results; the annual-report standalone P&L instead shows ₹45.2821 crore. That ₹0.0018 crore vintage difference is retained, not silently harmonized. Green Energy is wholly owned and Solar Solutions is 60% owned. No acquired-revenue bridge is assumed. A26 consolidation uses AS 21, and construction accounting references AS 7. These are Accounting Standards (AS) under the Companies (Accounting Standards) Rules, not assumed Ind AS. [E19]\\n- Latest audited balance-sheet date: 31 March 2026. H2 FY26 is a balancing half-year, not a standalone quarter. H1 FY27 results were not available by the cutoff. No annualization of a seasonal half-year or post-cutoff result is used.\\n- Horizon: five years from 19 October 2026, plus a one-year terminal funding test. Equity hurdle: 15% nominal INR before personal tax; sensitivities 12% and 18%. Analytical nominal INR discount range: 12–18%, distinct from the return hurdle. The primary illustrative discount rate is 15%.\\n- Liquidity reference: ₹5 lakh, not a recommended position. Brokerage, taxes and market impact are not reliably quantifiable; a 2% round-trip execution cost would reduce a positive five-year wealth multiple by 2%, and is not embedded as a fictitious exact expense.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason and evidence required to resolve it |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 Integrity/minority protection | Hold — unresolved | The use-of-proceeds verification and control contradictions require reconciliation. No substantiated diversion or severe minority abuse was established; do not convert an audit qualification into a fraud allegation. Auditor-backed funds-use schedule and control remediation required. |\\n| G2 Financial reliability | Hold — unresolved | Material unbilled revenue, opaque advances, conflicting audit/board assertions, historical reclassifications and EPS discrepancies. IAS remains NS; numerical quality rankings are also excluded. Independently verified billing/collections, reconciled statements and auditor closure required. |\\n| G3 Funding/survival | Hold — unresolved | Current cash, drawing power, covenants and post-March debt are unverified. Credible linked stresses require rescue; this is not evidence that an actual default already occurred. Updated lender statements, maturity ladder and collections required. |\\n| G4 Tradability | Conditional | All 60 sessions traded, but spreads, executable depth and current surveillance restrictions remain unverified. Lot and practical participation constrain implementation. |\\n\\nConfidence is High that the specified primary disclosures and exchange records say what is recorded; Medium on business description, concentration history and persistent cash weakness; Low on reliability of current recognized earnings, asset recoverability, incremental returns and investable valuation. Overall conclusion confidence is Low because the weakest material subject is financial reliability. A reproducible model is not proof its economic assumptions are true.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\n| Measure | Known sum | Coverage | Raw | Raw mathematical bound | Cap | Final / capped bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | 2.86 | 76.00% | NS | ['3.10', '5.26'] | None | NS / ['3.10', '5.26'] |\\n| GQS | 1.20 | 30.00% | NS | ['1.90', '8.20'] | 5 | NS / ['1.90', '5.00'] |\\n| IAS | 1.00 | 30.00% | NS | ['1.70', '8.00'] | None | NS / ['1.70', '8.00'] |\\n\\nThe NS aggregates are not zero and their missing weights are not redistributed. Bounds are mathematical possibilities, not forecasts or confidence intervals. IAS additionally propagates the narrower BQS bound rather than treating unknown BQS as any number from 1 to 10; its exact propagated interval is 2.225–6.815, retained unrounded in score_audit.json. No midpoint is ranked.\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence | Basis and contrary evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | E03, E09, E10, E11, E16 | Recurring statutory delays, historical internal-audit absence and contradictory proceeds-use reporting materially weaken stewardship. Remuneration escalated despite negative CFO. Contrary: No fraud or default was reported. Independent directors, audit-trail/IFC reports and promoter collateral provide contrary support. No substantiated diversion, so no automatic GOV ≤ 3 or G1 failure. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium | E12, E13, E14 | Maintenance track record and customer qualification help win repeat work, but competitive tenders and customer/project concentration prevent verified pricing power. Contrary: The multiyear ONGC relationship and execution experience may support entry barriers; excess cash returns and a defined market share are unproven. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | E02, E03, E20 | Reported five- and three-year CAGRs reach the 9 starting anchor, but ₹107.77 crore latest unbilled revenue and changing gross/WIP mix prevent a reliable underlying-growth score. NS is not an arbitrary penalty. Contrary: Even qualification-adjusted revenue implies substantial multiyear expansion. No assumption is made that all growth is fabricated. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium | E12, E13, E14, E15 | Recurring maintenance and a long ONGC relationship create accessible demand. Scaling remains constrained by cash, production and concentrated Epitome execution. Contrary: Semiconductor TAM is excluded; established customer relationships and new field work offer genuine opportunities. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | E04, E05, E07, E18 | Large FY26 ONGC and land investments, and ₹110.96 crore other advances, prevent attribution of incremental returns. Aggregate proxies cannot validate cohort economics. Contrary: Reported EBIT/NOPAT growth is positive and some assets are commissioned; this is not proof that capital was destroyed. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Medium | E05, E06, E08 | Four consecutive negative-CFO years, cumulative collection deficits and older receivables despite large customer/supplier funding show persistent poor realization. Contrary: Some absorption funds genuine growth. The conclusion survives all retained CFO vintages and is not merely one year of capex. |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | E07, E06, E18 | Cash of ₹6.58 crore versus debt of ₹142.22 crore, large demand/bill exposure and guarantees imply funding fragility. The model requires rapid collections or optional credit. Contrary: No default was reported and the June 2026 rating improved to BBB+/A2. A rating is not verified drawable headroom. G3 is a hold, not proven actual insolvency. |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium | E12, E13, E03 | Revenue, equipment and workover milestones are real, but Epitome timing and unbilled-certification/mainboard promises remain unresolved. Higher throughput is not equivalent to collection. Contrary: Ten CWP workovers were reported complete. H2 weakness was attributed to supply-chain issues, but independent milestone cash acceptance is missing. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | E02, E03, E04, E10 | Reported PAT is largely operating, yet a ₹13.1163 crore qualification impact and inconsistent EPS prevent reliable normalized per-share growth. Contrary: Other income is only ₹0.5136 crore, dilution is bounded and current issued capital is verified. |\\n| R | NS | Low | E18, E03, E07 | There is no defensible unconditional base IRR because of pre-entry cash failures and unresolved financial reliability. Conditional scenarios are illustrations only. Contrary: The optional-finance bull case works under strong collection and margin assumptions, but is not a committee-ready base case. |\\n| D | 2 | Medium | E03, E05, E07, E18 | A credible permanent-loss path arises from uncertified work, uncollected advances and senior claims. Returns are suppressed after any cash breach. Contrary: Reported net assets and customer support could recover value. NS recovery price is not an assertion of zero recovery. |\\n| P | NS | Low | E04, E18 | Low reported P/E cannot establish implied expectations until earnings and funding are reliable. Reverse trials test conditional required economics. Contrary: The quotation implies less than the strong bull case, but unverified asset quality dominates the multiple. |\\n| L | 6 | Medium | E01, E17 | The 2.43-session exit at 5% participation starts at score 7. An explicit one-point downgrade for unverified spread/depth and SME lot/circuit execution gives 6. Contrary: Every one of 60 sessions traded, and a ₹46,280 lot fits the reference position. A lower price is not evidence of absent liquidity. |\\n\\nCanonical weights: BQS GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%; GQS REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%; IAS BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%. Every multiplication is retained in score_audit.json with Decimal precision40; displays use conventional half-up one-decimal rounding. BQS is reused unrounded in IAS when scoreable. No extra arbitrary risk haircut is applied.\\n\\nCASH=2 reflects persistent poor realization, so the explicit GQS cap is 5, while GQS stays NS. GOV=4 does not trigger the governance cap; G1 is not an evidenced failure. G3 remains a hold, not an evidenced funding failure, so no invented IAS funding cap is applied. G2 alone is sufficient to prevent a favorable investment conclusion. Moving GOV and DUR separately by ±1 is retained: GOV's hypothetical fall to 3 triggers the explicit governance cap but does not turn NS into a point score. The hypothetical GOV = 3 case caps both BQS and IAS at 3, with nested IAS bounds propagated after the BQS cap; all missing aggregates remain NS. This is a sensitivity, not a misconduct finding. REV's reported CAGR starting band would be 9; latest recognition uncertainty prevents assigning that score. Historical 9–10 scores also require a persistent cycle, not a mechanically large CAGR.\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\nRBM sells engineering, procurement, construction, fabrication, repair, shutdown and maintenance services to process-industry clients. Mechanical/rotary equipment, piping, tanks, structures, electrical/instrumentation and related site services are the operational context. Customers buy safety, qualification, timely mobilization and reliable execution, but still negotiate price and milestone acceptance. The contractor pays employees, subcontractors, materials and machinery providers before final cash collection. Contract progress may become recognized revenue, then certified bills, then collected cash; these stages must not be conflated. [IPO business section; A26 accounting policies; C26]\\n\\nFY26 has a single disclosed reportable segment. There is no verified segmented profitability or capital-return schedule for EPC versus maintenance versus upstream work. Materials consumed ₹119.2243 crore, net WIP credit ₹7.0495 crore and subcontractor charges ₹188.9650 crore illustrate the large bought-in execution component. Employee costs ₹83.9439 crore include ₹3 crore directors' remuneration. Gross profit/value added is ND because direct labor, site, subcontractor and overhead allocations are not reliably separable. The P&L's operating EBITDA is not gross profit. [A26 notes22–28; E02]\\n\\nThe ONGC Nandej production-enhancement contract adds a different economic risk: field production, equipment availability, development spend, oil-price/FX assumptions and revenue-sharing mechanics. The 15-year term, possible five-year extension and headline ₹3,497 crore contract value are not a near-term receivable. Management describes base-production fees and a share of incremental production; the exact monthly-versus-daily minimum-production language in the call is insufficiently consistent to construct a bankable well-level model. No blanket annual revenue division of the headline contract is used. [C26 pp7–9; CRA25 p2]\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\nMechanism: repeated safe site execution and customer qualification can lower a customer's switching appetite. Evidence: continued maintenance relationships, named industrial customers and the ONGC work programme. Durability: useful but dependent on execution, key staff and competitive tendering. Failure mode: delays, accidents, inadequate performance, customer concentration, aggressive bids or inability to fund mobilization. Large clients do not remove certification delays or bargaining power. [E12–E14]\\n\\nMechanism: owning specialized ONGC-related equipment could improve control and reduce hired-equipment expense. Evidence: net ONGC assets of ₹71.0846 crore appear in FY26 PPE. Durability: returns depend on utilization and production, not merely equipment ownership. Failure mode: underperforming wells or imported parts, weak production sharing, large replacement costs or capital tied to one field. No superior unit economics have yet been established. [A26 PPE note12/13; C26]\\n\\nThe 2022 prospectus disclosed 88.06% of FY22 sales from five customers, versus 88.32% in FY21 and 98.70% in FY20; FY26 customer revenue shares are ND. The May 2025 rating rationale attributed 75% of the ₹1,245.85 crore EPC backlog to Epitome and separately described ONGC. Gujarat contributed approximately 88% of FY24 revenue. Do not add these different denominators into a single spurious concentration measure. Supplier concentration was also disclosed, but the prospectus's period ordering is inconsistent; the clear lesson is concentrated sourcing, not a precisely comparable current percentage. [IPO risks; CRA25]\\n\\n| Comparator | Relevant economic comparison | Important difference |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Engineers India | Engineering, refinery/petrochemical EPC and project management; a more established technical/qualification benchmark | Consultancy, design and technology ownership differ from labor/subcontract-heavy site execution. The official profile documents engineering capacity and process technologies; not a direct multiple transfer. |\\n| Deep Industries | Equipment-led oilfield services and a 15-year ONGC production-enhancement contract | Closer upstream capital/production comparator, but charter-hire/service mix and group assets differ. Its FY26 chairman letter confirms active field development, not a guarantee of RBM's economics. |\\n| K2 Infragen | Smaller-company EPC/turnkey, power and project engineering | A process/funding-risk comparator rather than proof of inferior or superior cash quality. No unverified peer score or current valuation multiple is assigned. |\\n\\nSources: PEER_EIL official profile; PEER_DEEP FY26 report p2; PEER_K2 official business description. This peer set frames the economics. A contemporaneous normalized numeric peer valuation is ND; the primary cross-check below instead uses RBM's qualification-adjusted earnings and net assets. No unsupported peer-average fair value is fabricated.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\n| Period | Start sales | End sales | Reported CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY23–FY26 | 83.19 | 492.22 | 80.87% |\\n| FY21–FY26 | 38.71 | 492.22 | 66.29% |\\n\\nTen-year CAGR is ND: eleven comparable observations were not located. The complete seven-year reported series and each annual rupee addition appear below. FY21 had a pandemic-affected loss, but its revenue was not a zero denominator. Three-year expansion is independently large; neither the small starting scale nor the pandemic label justifies hiding the absolute additions.\\n\\n| FY | Revenue growth | ₹ addition | EBITDA margin | PAT margin | Average AR days | ROIC proxy | ROCE proxy | ROE | Current-share EPS |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY20 | NS | NS | 2.48% | -0.34% | NS | NS | NS | NS | -0.11 |\\n| FY21 | 10.33% | 3.62 | -1.10% | -3.44% | 146.46 | -7.83% | -7.61% | -26.57% | -1.25 |\\n| FY22 | 22.80% | 8.83 | 7.18% | 4.02% | 119.24 | 16.12% | 20.91% | 34.23% | 1.80 |\\n| FY23 | 74.98% | 35.65 | 5.64% | 2.66% | 74.67 | 14.78% | 19.51% | 18.60% | 2.08 |\\n| FY24 | 55.94% | 46.53 | 14.54% | 8.55% | 54.27 | 22.58% | 28.20% | 19.38% | 10.43 |\\n| FY25 | 148.02% | 192.02 | 13.35% | 9.16% | 49.47 | 25.66% | 32.39% | 24.75% | 27.72 |\\n| FY26 | 52.98% | 170.47 | 14.95% | 9.20% | 66.28 | 21.54% | 27.98% | 27.56% | 42.58 |\\n\\nFY26 consolidated sales rose 53.00%, but H2 sales ₹208.2226 crore fell 4.65% against H2 FY25 ₹218.3751 crore after H1 sales rose strongly. H1 FY26 sales ₹283.9941 crore compares with derived H1 FY25 ₹103.3707 crore. Half-year profit also slowed: H2 ₹18.3615 crore versus ₹19.5677 crore. The two halves should not be interpreted as four comparable quarters. [R26 results]\\n\\nGrowth bridge: new EPC scope, more materials/subcontracted work, a larger operating footprint and the first upstream assets plausibly explain significant expansion. No material acquired-revenue contribution was disclosed in the reviewed group statements, but that does not establish an audited organic/constant-price/volume bridge. Historical subsidiaries and consistent revenue perimeter help comparability; exact price, volume, new-customer and mix contributions are ND. FY26's ₹107.7677 crore unbilled amount equals a material part of annual sales. Deducting it produces alternative revenue of about ₹384.45 crore, not ₹384.96 crore: the latter is total income including other income. This alternative is an audit-impact diagnostic, not a revised clean revenue series. [E04; E20]\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nDemand access is credible in maintenance, turnaround and process-industry projects, while upstream production adds duration. Accessible growth is constrained by execution cash, not by a broad national infrastructure TAM. There is no verified company market share or defined addressable-market denominator, so implied market share remains ND rather than being inferred from a promotional national spending figure.\\n\\nThe modeled five-year revenue paths are cash-linked execution envelopes, not backlog multiplication: bear ₹380/390/410/440/470 crore; base ₹550/630/710/790/860 crore; bull ₹650/780/920/1,060/1,200 crore. Base FY1 is below management's ₹700 crore FY27 target and recognizes slower collection/execution. Each path funds receivables, new unbilled work, WIP, supplier advances, retention and fixed assets, while customer advances and payables provide explicit, bounded operating support. A ₹100 crore increment at base assumptions requires operating receivables of ₹26.03 crore, WIP ₹15.5 crore, supplier advances ₹14 crore, new unbilled ₹6.85 crore, retention ₹0.7 crore and deposits ₹1 crore, offset by payables ₹25 crore, customer advances ₹22 crore and accruals ₹2.4 crore. Incremental fixed capital is another ₹23 crore before maintenance. These are assumptions, not disclosed contract terms. [model_inputs.json]\\n\\nThis shows why growth and free cash are not interchangeable. Full reinvestment accompanies each model increase, not merely an EPS uplift. The simple growth ≈ reinvestment-rate × incremental-return identity cannot validate the forecast because incremental return is NS. The model nevertheless rejects terminal valuation where replacement/growth investment and cash survival cannot coexist.\\n\\nFY26 PPE additions include land ₹30.8105 crore and ONGC gross additions ₹74.406 crore, alongside other equipment. The ₹13.6565 crore prior development/CWIP amount transfers through the PPE schedule; adding it to cash capex again would double count. Net PPE/intangibles ₹113.154 crore is not the same as gross block. The call's shorthand “gross block ₹113.11 crore” matches net tangible PPE rather than the full gross schedule. [A26 PPE schedule; C26]\\n\\nSemiconductor optionality is excluded from base operating revenue and valuation. April's LOI/MoU requires diligence, approvals and definitive agreements; a stated FDI commitment is not proof cash is available to RBM's existing creditors. Its 30% RBM/70% partner proposed equity conflicts with some June-call phrasing about promoters holding 70%; an initial ₹60 crore land/infrastructure discussion conflicts with deferring contribution for three to four years. The sensitivity therefore tests actual ₹60 crore and ₹300 crore outflows without giving credit for speculative semiconductor proceeds. A separate definitive funding model would be required before treating this as value. [MOU; C26; E15]\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\nThe promoter family owns approximately 62.50% at March 2026. The FY24 preferential issue added 1,662,000 shares at ₹386, gross ₹64.1532 crore; 530,000 promoter warrants at the same price had total consideration ₹20.458 crore and converted on 12 August 2025. ₹1.1535 crore of the warrant money came in FY26; do not add the entire conversion price as new cash again. Current shares are 25.97% above FY23's 8.441 million, excluding the earlier bonus effect. FY24 warrants/reserves were subsequently reclassified without changing total equity. The original IPO was 2,325,000 fresh shares for ₹8.37 crore. [IPO; A24 CARO; A25 warrant note; A26 share capital]\\n\\nWarrant utilization remains a verification issue, not proof of theft: the FY26 main opinion says supporting information was not furnished, while CARO10(b) says the proceeds were used for the intended purpose. The board's listing/trading-approval explanation establishes admission of shares, not how money was spent. June's statement that a utilization certificate was filed and September's “subsequently billed” assertion are management claims; neither independently removes the signed audit qualification. The board describes the unbilled amount as relating to September 2024, whereas the FY26 auditor refers to the year ended March 2026 and the impact form says half-year. Preserve this period conflict. [A26 pp23,45,52,86; R26; C26]\\n\\nFY26 directors' remuneration of ₹3 crore versus ₹0.7325 crore in the comparative payroll line is a substantial increase. A spouse received ₹0.6 crore salary, and family rents total about ₹0.3906 crore. Directors' remuneration plus spouse salary is about 7.95% of reported consolidated PAT and much more material against negative CFO. The notes also show ₹5.597 crore interest-free promoter loans without specific terms, and promoter/relative collateral backing facilities. That support is economically useful, but it is repayable funding and potential concentration, not free equity. FY25 related-party notes show promoter loans given/recovered; the bridge to all subsequent balances and terms needs fuller explanation. [A26 related-party notes]\\n\\nThe IPO restatement also has material reliability limitations: F9 describes gratuity and leave on a pay-as-you-go basis and says profit is overstated by an unquantified amount; F10 makes loans, creditors, debtors and advances subject to confirmation, with effects unascertainable. Restatement auditor Raichura relied on predecessor SSRS/Sarvesh audits rather than routinely re-auditing them. The statement that no qualifications required restatement adjustment is not blanket proof of clean books. [IPO F9–F10]\\n\\nAuditor/control history is not summarized as “clean”: FY23 and FY24 main opinions and IFC opinions were unmodified, but FY24 CARO said a required internal-audit system was absent, described no inventory despite significant WIP, and reported bank-return reconciliation differences. FY25 had joint MB Jajodia/Patel Jain signers, a prior statutory-auditor resignation noted in CARO, overdue income-tax/TDS and recurring bank-return differences. FY26 continued statutory-payment irregularity but reported none over six months and no loan default; unmodified IFC is not an assurance on recoverability of each balance. The FY26 impact form's “second time” qualification does not override FY25's actual unmodified main opinion. [A23–A26 full audit/CARO/IFC]\\n\\n| Dated commitment / milestone | Evidence by cutoff | Assessment |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Epitome project funded/on schedule in March 2024 and May 2025 rationales | June 2026 call discusses delays, large machinery advances and later completion timing | Order value is not executed cash; full original/updated schedule and certified work ledger needed. |\\n| ONGC CWP, February–May 2025 | July 2025 issuer release reports ten workovers completed within timetable | Positive execution milestone, management reported; not a cash-collection certificate. |\\n| June 2026: unbilled certification expected by June; warrant certificate said filed | September annual report still contains signed qualified opinions; board says billed | Unresolved independent closure. |\\n| June 2026: mainboard migration in about 20–25 days | Official 19 October master remains SM with 200-share lot | Promise not verified as delivered; no invented migration date. |\\n| June 2026: no parent dilution next six to eight months | Observed October issued shares remain 10.633 million | Consistent to cutoff; does not preapprove or fund later equity needs. |\\n| April semiconductor definitive agreements within 90 days | June still early-stage discussion; no verified fully funded close by cutoff | Do not treat proposed project as operating asset or committed rescue. |\\n\\nThe September CFO resignation cites other professional commitments and says no other material reason; an interim CFO was appointed. Internal-auditor changes in July/August are another continuity item. No adverse resignation motive is invented. [CFO; E16]\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\n| FY | Revenue | Operating EBITDA | EBIT | PAT | Normalized PAT proxy | CFO | Cash capex | FCF |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY20 | 35.09 | 0.87 | 0.43 | -0.12 | -0.14 | 0.84 | 2.11 | -1.27 |\\n| FY21 | 38.71 | -0.43 | -0.94 | -1.33 | -1.54 | -0.36 | 0.58 | -0.94 |\\n| FY22 | 47.54 | 3.41 | 2.91 | 1.91 | 1.78 | 0.83 | 1.02 | -0.19 |\\n| FY23 | 83.19 | 4.69 | 3.94 | 2.21 | 2.26 | -4.31 | 2.08 | -6.38 |\\n| FY24 | 129.73 | 18.86 | 17.59 | 11.09 | 11.80 | -51.74 | 2.57 | -54.31 |\\n| FY25 | 321.75 | 42.95 | 40.77 | 29.47 | 29.46 | -12.16 | 21.28 | -33.44 |\\n| FY26 | 492.22 | 73.57 | 67.46 | 45.28 | 46.09 | -25.58 | 94.82 | -120.40 |\\n\\nReported numbers are retained, not silently replaced by adjusted estimates. Normalized PAT proxy removes all other income and applies an analytical 25% tax to positive PBT; it does not resolve unbilled recognition, provisions or owner attribution. Therefore normalized EARN remains NS. Current-share EPS rebases group PAT to today's 10.633 million shares for comparison, not historical statutory EPS. The original diluted EPS, current-share EPS and inconsistent FY26 filing EPS are separate fields. No non-recurring profits were fabricated to improve the normalization.\\n\\n| Window | CFO | PAT | CFO/PAT | FCF | Cash after capex and finance |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 3y | -89.48 | 85.84 | -1.04 | -208.15 | -217.51 |\\n| 4y | -93.78 | 88.05 | -1.07 | -214.53 | -224.81 |\\n| 5y | -92.95 | 89.96 | -1.03 | -214.72 | -225.54 |\\n\\nCumulative CFO/PAT is sum(CFO)/sum(PAT), never an average of annual ratios. All reported vintages remain weak: FY23 CFO changes from −₹3.9783 to −₹4.3059 crore; FY24 from −₹50.4387 to −₹51.7387 crore; FY25 from −₹3.4409 to −₹12.1637 crore. The FY24 change reallocates ₹1.3 crore between investments and advances. FY25 reallocates about ₹8.7227 crore of FD release into investing and also changes deposit, advance and gratuity presentation. Cash totals are unaffected apart from disclosed rounding. The original FY25 cash components call ₹5.6622 crore a fixed account, while the next comparative presents substantially the same amount as current-bank cash. The model therefore excludes separate long-maturity FD ₹11.8231 crore and tests making most nominal cash unavailable. [E08; retained vintage ledger]\\n\\nFY26's cash bridge is especially instructive: ₹74.2013 crore operating cash before working-capital changes was outweighed by ₹44.2261 crore receivables growth, ₹7.0495 crore WIP and ₹229.5558 crore advances. Trade-payable growth ₹114.7399 crore and other current liabilities ₹72.2126 crore partly funded that absorption. Other noncurrent assets, tax-provision movement, gratuity payment and cash tax complete reported CFO −₹25.5758 crore. Then ₹94.824 crore cash capex, ₹5.1165 crore further FD investment and interest drain require new debt. Gross new borrowings ₹131.375 crore, ₹1.1575 crore equity/minority receipts and other items left only ₹0.8741 crore net cash increase. Every term is retained; CFO is not charged working capital twice. Interest ₹6.0119 crore is financing in the cash-flow statement, so the after-finance FCF proxy deducts it separately. [A26 consolidated cash flow]\\n\\nCollection quality: billed receivables ₹111.4963 crore include ₹24.3173 crore more than a year past due, versus ₹2.0835 crore a year earlier. Balances older than six months are ₹26.8581 crore, with no doubtful-debt provision. The notes mark amounts good based on management certification. Separate unbilled ₹107.7678 crore and retention ₹3.4157 crore make the billed-only debtor-day ratio incomplete; at year-end, billed plus unbilled plus retention is roughly 165 days of reported sales. WIP ₹76.1745 crore and supplier advances ₹68.8794 crore add execution exposure. “Other loans and advances” ₹110.9565 crore require counterparties, ageing, security and recoverability evidence before being treated as liquid or productive operating capital. [A26 notes18–21; E05–E06]\\n\\nFunding: gross debt ₹142.2245 crore includes ₹59.9415 crore noncurrent debt and ₹82.283 crore short-term debt/current maturities. The latter includes bank/NBFC cash credit, ₹33.4495 crore vendor-bill discounting, ₹10 crore intercorporate borrowing and ₹7.6487 crore current term maturities. Bill discounting is financing, not cash conversion. The apparent net-debt/EBITDA ratio around 1.84x and EBIT/finance-cost around 11.22x look comfortable only if qualified earnings are reliable and year-average interest approximates the much larger closing debt; neither is enough to establish resilience. Rates include 9–10% secured facilities and 14–16% unsecured business loans. [A26 debt notes]\\n\\nFD collateral requirements of 20%/30%, hypothecated assets and an ONGC receivables escrow matter to fungibility. ₹8.8925 crore bank guarantees, ₹0.9478 crore disputed income-tax claims and other statutory disputes/dues are not ignored. The June 2026 rating upgrade to BBB+/Stable and A2 on ₹106.90 crore facilities is supportive but does not certify incremental drawable availability, nor reconcile every borrowing in the March balance sheet. The latest complete rationale located was May 2025; its historically adequate liquidity and 87% cash-credit utilization should not be carried forward over a twelvefold increase in debt. May 2025’s rationale table gives FY24 debt as ₹10.60 crore, versus ₹2.1911 crore in the audited FY24/FY25 statements; absent a reconciliation, the model uses audited debt, not the rating table. A non-cooperation footnote in that document does not override its explicit “Nil” status and upgrade, so no non-cooperation allegation is made. [CRA25; CRA26; A26]\\n\\n| Window | Δ NOPAT | Δ financing IC | Incremental proxy | Δ PPE | Δ other IC |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1y | 20.02 | 176.65 | 11.33% | 88.71 | 87.94 |\\n| 3y | 47.64 | 298.12 | 15.98% | 109.12 | 189.00 |\\n| 5y | 51.54 | 311.06 | 16.57% | 110.96 | 200.11 |\\n\\nBroad operating IC ₹285.4526 crore versus financing IC ₹323.2749 crore, residual ₹37.8223 crore. Detailed component inputs are retained in historical_inputs.json; tax/GST, restricted FDs and other liabilities explain the definition gap, not a hidden return on new plant.\\n\\nROIC uses operating EBIT after analytical tax over average financing-based IC; ROCE uses EBIT over average equity-plus-debt. These are diagnostic proxies with an explicit perimeter, not interchangeable metrics. Financing IC includes warrant funds as capital already supplied; restricted cash and tax balances create definition differences. The large FY26 investment/advances and uncertain cost/revenue matching mean the observed incremental proxy cannot identify the return on ONGC assets, Epitome or new land. A reasonable nominal INR cost-of-capital range of 12–18% is an assumption; CAP is NS rather than falsely concluding that a noisy proxy proves excess returns. Maintenance capex is tested at 1.5%, 2.5% and 4% of sales; depreciation is not automatically maintenance spending.\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\n₹231.40 × 10,633,000 shares = ₹246.04762 crore. Reported P/E 5.43x; qualification-adjusted P/E 7.65x; P/B 1.31x. March-anchored EV ₹381.70 crore and EV/operating EBITDA 5.19x are not verified current enterprise value. 60-session mean/median traded value ₹6558712.83/₹4108965.00; 20-session mean/median ₹4053240.00/₹3545500.00. At 5% participation, ₹5 lakh needs 2.4337 sessions; at 2%, 6.0843, and at 10%, 1.2169. All 60 sessions traded. One 200-share lot costs ₹46,280; 10 lots ₹462,800; 11 lots ₹509,080.\\n\\nThe qualification-adjusted P/E and book value are cross-checks, not investable floors. The impact form's adjusted group PAT ₹32.1592 crore is about ₹13.1163 crore below reported PAT; it includes cost and tax matching, so subtracting the entire unbilled revenue from PAT would be wrong. Its adjusted net assets are ₹174.5125 crore, but the large uncertain advances and senior claims mean no credible liquidation value per share is established. There is no fair-value point estimate or unconditional buy-below price.\\n\\nThe core cash-linked paths are:\\n\\n| Case | First breach | Entry cash | New shares | Terminal price | 5Y return |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear / no_new_finance | 2026-04-30 cycle1 supplier/payroll payment | -175.07 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| bear / bounded_optional | 2026-04-30 cycle4 supplier/payroll payment | -156.29 | 5762028 | NS | NS |\\n| base / no_new_finance | 2026-04-30 cycle1 supplier/payroll payment | -42.69 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| base / bounded_optional | 2026-06-30 cycle1 supplier/payroll payment | -23.76 | 4587853 | NS | NS |\\n| bull / no_new_finance | 2026-04-30 cycle1 supplier/payroll payment | 46.01 | 0 | NS | NS |\\n| bull / bounded_optional | None | 48.43 | 0 | 1340.05 | 42.09% |\\n\\n“Entry cash” is an assumed reconstruction from the last audited March balance sheet, not actual October cash. A negative diagnostic bridge means those assumptions cannot describe a funded going concern without additional evidence or finance; it does not assert the issuer actually ran out of cash on that historical date. Apart from the explicit opening cash-availability event on 31 March 2026, each event date is the monthly accounting endpoint, with ordered within-period payments/receipts, not a known bank-statement transaction date. New emergency funding accrues interest from the next period, reflecting assumed month-end draws; this is a limitation, not a claim that intramonth borrowing is free or conservative.\\n\\nModel mechanics are fully reconstructible:\\n\\n1. March cash ₹6.5784 crore, debt ₹142.2245 crore and operating balances initialize seven pre-entry periods through 19 October. Prospective entry cash is never reset to March cash. Pre-entry equity is prohibited because October's share count is verified.\\n2. Monthly modeled suppliers/payroll are paid before receipts in four settlement cycles; one/two/eight cycles are stressed. Cash taxes, capex, interest, term repayments, statutory settlement and optional guarantee calls have separate cash events. First breach and every later diagnostic event remain in the ledger.\\n3. No-new-finance paths require ₹74.6343 crore demand/bill/intercorporate debt to settle at the analytical one-year boundary. Actual on-demand call timing may be earlier. Bounded-optional paths assume renewal instead, not an imaginary new cash inflow. Existing revolver rollover remains conditional throughout the forecast and terminal year; the additional optional bank balance must be repaid in the terminal-year cash test. Incremental bank funding is capped at ₹20 crore, a hypothetical limit, not approved headroom inferred from the credit rating.\\n4. After entry only, equity may cover a cash shortfall at ₹173.55 per share, a 25% issue discount, 3% fees, ₹100 crore gross cap and authorized-share headroom. New shares round UP to whole shares needed but remain within the gross cap. Receipts and fees immediately affect cash; shares immediately affect dilution. Failed pre-entry paths never become ordinary return paths merely because a later rescue occurs.\\n5. Base assumptions collect 50% of old unbilled balances over three years and 25% of other advances over two years; bull uses 100% and 75%, bear zero. Normal new unbilled work is separately funded. Remaining old assets are not added as free terminal cash. No FD release, tax refund or semiconductor funding is invented.\\n6. Terminal value is earned FCFF6/(discount rate−growth), after replacement capex, growth fixed capital and operating working capital. Senior debt, NCI, required cash and any tax-reserve sensitivity are deducted once. A further year tests payment ordering, maturities and no additional financing. Failed cash/terminal tests suppress ordinary terminal price and IRR rather than show a meaningless EPS-based return.\\n\\nThe only core path that can produce a conditional ordinary return relies on strong collections and bull economics. It is not a supported base case. Its high numerical price is a warning about sensitivity, not a recommendation: the retained cash and low terminal capital intensity must actually be earned, while the model has no verified order-level margins or advances recovery. Output also decomposes price change into earnings growth × share dilution × implied terminal multiple; the multiple is derived from cash capitalization, not separately assumed rerating. No dividends are added to retained terminal cash.\\n\\nTwo-way operating sensitivity, keeping the bull execution/collection bridge and varying post-entry EBIT margin and billed-receivable days:\\n\\n| EBIT margin / DSO | Cash survives | Price | Return |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| margin=.06 / DSO=60 | True | 469.65 | 15.21% |\\n| margin=.06 / DSO=75 | True | 411.33 | 12.19% |\\n| margin=.06 / DSO=95 | True | 333.37 | 7.58% |\\n| margin=.06 / DSO=130 | True | 182.96 | -4.59% |\\n| margin=.08 / DSO=60 | True | 680.02 | 24.06% |\\n| margin=.08 / DSO=75 | True | 621.70 | 21.85% |\\n| margin=.08 / DSO=95 | True | 543.73 | 18.63% |\\n| margin=.08 / DSO=130 | True | 367.46 | 9.69% |\\n| margin=.10 / DSO=60 | True | 890.39 | 30.93% |\\n| margin=.10 / DSO=75 | True | 832.06 | 29.17% |\\n| margin=.10 / DSO=95 | True | 754.10 | 26.65% |\\n| margin=.10 / DSO=130 | True | 558.85 | 19.28% |\\n| margin=.12 / DSO=60 | True | 1100.75 | 36.60% |\\n| margin=.12 / DSO=75 | True | 1042.43 | 35.13% |\\n| margin=.12 / DSO=95 | True | 964.47 | 33.04% |\\n| margin=.12 / DSO=130 | True | 758.61 | 26.80% |\\n| margin=.14 / DSO=60 | True | 1311.12 | 41.47% |\\n| margin=.14 / DSO=75 | True | 1252.80 | 40.19% |\\n| margin=.14 / DSO=95 | True | 1174.83 | 38.40% |\\n| margin=.14 / DSO=130 | True | 967.32 | 33.12% |\\n| margin=.16 / DSO=60 | True | 1521.48 | 45.74% |\\n| margin=.16 / DSO=75 | True | 1463.16 | 44.61% |\\n| margin=.16 / DSO=95 | True | 1385.20 | 43.03% |\\n| margin=.16 / DSO=130 | True | 1176.27 | 38.43% |\\n\\nDiscount-rate/terminal-growth sensitivity, with explicit ongoing reinvestment:\\n\\n| Discount / terminal growth | Survives | Price | Return |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| discount=.12 / terminalg=0 | True | 1357.92 | 42.46% |\\n| discount=.12 / terminalg=.02 | True | 1546.20 | 46.21% |\\n| discount=.12 / terminalg=.03 | True | 1671.09 | 48.50% |\\n| discount=.12 / terminalg=.04 | True | 1826.74 | 51.17% |\\n| discount=.15 / terminalg=0 | True | 1155.72 | 37.94% |\\n| discount=.15 / terminalg=.02 | True | 1269.44 | 40.56% |\\n| discount=.15 / terminalg=.03 | True | 1340.05 | 42.09% |\\n| discount=.15 / terminalg=.04 | True | 1423.15 | 43.81% |\\n| discount=.18 / terminalg=0 | True | 1020.92 | 34.56% |\\n| discount=.18 / terminalg=.02 | True | 1096.47 | 36.50% |\\n| discount=.18 / terminalg=.03 | True | 1141.42 | 37.60% |\\n| discount=.18 / terminalg=.04 | True | 1192.53 | 38.81% |\\n\\nOther sensitivity rows independently vary cash availability, pre-entry capex, financing cap and price, statutory/tax reserve, maintenance spending, interest, guarantee call, old advances and semiconductor cash outflow. Terminal examples that fail a cash test remain NS, not zero recovery. The accessible minimum-input package reconstructs all 247 unique full paths and 371 trial/label references, including failed root attempts; identical candidates are deduplicated by complete effective assumptions without dropping any reference.\\n\\nReverse margin requirements:\\n\\n| Target | Status | Required EBIT margin bracket | Target terminal price |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| hurdle12 | cash-feasible margin root | 5.97%–5.97% | 407.81 |\\n| hurdle15 | cash-feasible margin root | 6.51%–6.51% | 465.43 |\\n| hurdle18 | cash-feasible margin root | 7.12%–7.12% | 529.39 |\\n| fivefold | cash-feasible margin root | 13.09%–13.09% | 1157.00 |\\n| tenfold | cash-feasible margin root | 24.09%–24.09% | 2314.00 |\\n\\nEach root uses the explicitly conditional bull revenue/collection path and bounds EBIT margin from 4% to 30%. It is a joint cash-feasibility and price test: a threshold where funding suddenly becomes feasible is not mislabeled a smooth valuation root. Bisection residuals, endpoint paths and every failed trial are saved. Uniform revenue-growth probes at 0/10/20/30/40/50% also rerun all capital and funding links. A root's existence is not evidence that management can deliver it.\\n\\nFor every surviving path, entry limits at 12%, 15% and 18% hurdles are terminal price divided by (1+hurdle)^5. For breached paths they are NS. There is no defensible unconditional entry-price range while G2 and G3 are unresolved. A 15% equity hurdle is not silently substituted for all discount-rate sensitivities.\\n\\n\"","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\nThe thesis-break variable is conversion of recognized project progress and advances into collectible cash. A credible linked loss path is: Epitome or upstream execution disappoints → certification slows and old advances fail to unwind → lower margins and larger WIP/unbilled/receivables → customer advances and supplier credit stop expanding → demand/bill lenders require repayment → discounted equity or asset sales become necessary. If rescue cannot be funded, ordinary shareholders are residual claimants after creditors and contractual obligations. A nominally profitable P&L can coexist with permanent capital impairment.\\n\\nThe strongest evidence against a uniformly pessimistic view is real operating scale growth, repeated industrial customers, tangible ONGC investment, reported workover milestones, no disclosed loan default and improved credit ratings. These facts justify research rather than a proven-fraud verdict. They do not answer why four years of profits did not translate into CFO, why large advances lack transparent terms or why the statutory qualification remains. [E02–E14]\\n\\nImpairment risk: High. The model quantifies funding failure, not a fictitious precise liquidation price. Bear-case quote downside is NS because cash survival fails and recovery cannot be established; this is more informative than treating a negative cash balance plus a generous P/E as a valid share price. Even a surviving optional case can suffer dilution, and shareholder return must use its enlarged share count. Mainboard migration or a new contract announcement alone would not repair this failure path.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\nFivefold price appreciation over five years requires 37.97% annualized price growth; tenfold requires 58.49%. At ₹231.40, terminal prices would need to be ₹1,157 and ₹2,314 respectively, before costs and personal tax. The reverse tests solve these targets with the same cash, reinvestment and bounded-financing model rather than multiplying headline orders by a P/E. A large terminal result can arise under the bull case, but would require simultaneous collections, margin, utilization and funding assumptions that are not verified.\\n\\nClassification: Speculative / unsupported, not “10× potential” as a forecast. A large order value spread over fifteen years is not equivalent to fivefold annual revenue. Semiconductor proposed project value is not parent equity value. No precise success probability or synthetic blended IRR is provided.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\nResearch checkpoints only; no recurring automation is created.\\n\\n| KPI / event | Baseline | Warning / thesis-break distinction | Review evidence |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Audit closure and unbilled collections | ₹107.7677 crore qualified; board says billed | Warning: billing without receipts. Thesis break: material reversal or independently confirmed non-recoverability | Next results, auditor certificate and customer/bank reconciliation |\\n| Other advances | ₹110.9565 crore, terms/counterparties not established | Warning: balances grow without schedules. Thesis break: unsupported third-party or related-party transfer/impairment | Note-level counterparties, ageing, security and cash recovery |\\n| CFO | FY26 −₹25.5758 crore; four negative years | Warning: further deficit despite collection promises. Break: credible obligations cannot be funded | Comparable cash-flow statements and bank bridge |\\n| Aged trade debtors | >1 year ₹24.3173 crore, nil provision | Warning: roll-forward into older buckets. Break: material loss with inadequate capital | Ageing and subsequent receipts |\\n| Demand/bill debt and liquidity | Debt ₹142.2245 crore; reported cash ₹6.5784 crore | Warning: utilization near limit or rising expensive debt. Break: lender call/default without committed coverage | Sanctions, drawing power, escrow/collateral and maturities |\\n| Epitome execution | Highly concentrated historical backlog, machinery advances | Warning: later certification/completion. Break: cancelled or uneconomic scope with trapped capital | Work certificates, variations, payments and updated schedule |\\n| ONGC economics | Net ONGC assets ₹71.0846 crore; long-duration PEC | Warning: production or equipment availability below plan. Break: sustainable cash returns below debt and replacement needs | Field production, fee/share receipts and segment capital ledger |\\n| Semiconductor commitments | Conditional LOI/MoU, no fully funded close verified | Warning: cash spend before finance close. Break: parent commits obligations it cannot fund | Definitive agreements, ownership and financing proof |\\n| Governance/control remediation | Recurrent bank-return/statutory issues; FY26 qualification | Warning: unresolved contradictions or late filing. Break: evidenced material diversion/minority abuse | Auditor/CARO, internal audit, RPT and compliance records |\\n| Tradability and dilution | SM, 200-share lot, 10.633 million shares, 62.5% promoter ownership at March | Warning: one-sided trades, new warrants or unsupported migration claim | Current NSE master, shareholding, order book and issue terms |\\n\\n\"","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\nHold — unresolved. Research priority is collection, accounting and funding verification, not promotional upside modeling. BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS with explicit coverage and bounds; no quality, growth or investment point-ranking is permitted. It fails the evidence-backed-compounder criteria because necessary factor and gate conditions are unfulfilled. This does not assert the business has no value or that all reported work is fictitious.\\n\\nThe useful next evidence is narrow: reconcile unbilled amount and corresponding costs by contract/date; document conversion into accepted invoices and bank receipts; explain ₹110.96 crore other advances; obtain a current debt/drawability schedule; reconcile all historical cash-flow and EPS changes; and verify warrant use with auditor support. Only then should a supported base-case IRR and IAS be reconsidered. For an existing holder, this is a reliability/funding/liquidity review candidate, not an automatic sell instruction without position size, tax context and executable market depth.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\n| ID | Document | Reviewed sections |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| A26 | [Annual report FY2026](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31867_RBMINFRA_2025_2026_A_3419639_08092026161118.pdf) (2026-09-08) | PDF pp23,45–55,56–83,86–123: board, full statutory audits/CARO/IFC, statements, accounting policies and notes |\\n| A25 | [Annual report FY2025](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_27740_RBMINFRA_2024_2025_A_17662749_26082025191926.pdf) (2025-08-26) | PDF pp35–44 full audit/CARO/IFC; pp45–75 statements/notes; board and secretarial annexures |\\n| A24 | [Annual report FY2024](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_25857_RBMINFRA_2023_2024_05092024175113.pdf) (2024-09-05) | Full statutory audit/CARO/IFC and financial statements; printed pp33–40 and43–73 |\\n| A23 | [Annual report FY2023](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_23313_RBMINFRA_2022_2023_02092023210105_09022023220003.zip) (2023-09-02) | Full audit/CARO/IFC and printed pp47–76 financials/notes |\\n| IPO | [IPO prospectus](https://rbminfracon.co.in/assets/img/07C.%20PROSPECTUS_RBM.pdf) (2022-12-13) | Risk factors printed pp21–22; business; restated financials F1–F34 including restatement reconciliation; PDF pp154–187 |\\n| R26 | [Revised FY2026 results and audit-qualification impact](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_26062026173117_Revised_Outcome_Signed.pdf) (2026-06-26) | Cover correction acknowledgement; full standalone/consolidated audit; impact statements PDF pp7–8 and19–20; half-year results |\\n| C26 | [FY2026 earnings-call transcript](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_19062026174620_CL_Transcript_Signed.pdf) (2026-06-19) | All 19 pages; particularly pp3–8 unbilled, advances, funding, ONGC structure and semiconductor claims |\\n| CRA24 | [Infomerics credit rationale](https://www.infomerics.com/admin/uploads/PR-RBM-Infracon-14032024.pdf) (2024-03-14) | All 7 pages including facility/covenant annexures; web-read official PDF; local download403 |\\n| CRA25 | [Infomerics credit rationale](https://www.infomerics.com/admin/uploads/Pr-RBM-Infra-13may25.pdf) (2025-05-13) | All 7 pages; backlog/concentration/liquidity and facilities |\\n| CRA26 | [Rating upgrade issuer disclosure](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_25062026193112_Disclosure_of_Rating_Signed.pdf) (2026-06-25) | One-page upgrade BBB+/Stable,A2 on ₹106.90 crore; not fresh lending commitment |\\n| MOU | [Semiconductor LOI/MOU disclosure](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_18042026183252_MOU_Signed.pdf) (2026-04-18) | All 4 pages; conditional definitive agreements/diligence within 90 days |\\n| CFO | [CFO resignation and interim appointment](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_25092026185505_Outcome_of_BM__Resignation_Letter_Signed.pdf) (2026-09-25) | All 5 pages incl resignation reason and new CFO profile |\\n| CWP | [ONGC work-programme completion](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/RBMINFRACON_03072025163211_press_release_Signed.pdf) (2025-07-03) | Management-reported10 workovers February 13–May 13, 2025; not independent cash certification |\\n| NSE | [Official NSE daily bhavcopies](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip) (2026-10-19) | 60 dated sessions, issuer rows only, matched exact ISIN across active series |\\n| MASTER | [Official NSE security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz) (2026-10-19) | Exact-ISIN security rows; SM active SM, 200-share lot, 10,633,000 shares |\\n| PEER_EIL | [Engineers India company profile](https://engineersindia.com/Profile) (Profile, accessed for cutoff research) | Consultancy/EPC scope and technology capabilities; qualitative comparator not scored |\\n| PEER_DEEP | [Deep Industries annual reports](https://www.deepindustries.com/docs/Deep_AR_25-26_Final.pdf) (FY2025–2026 report, available before cutoff) | FY26 chairman letter, PDF p2: charter-hire equipment and 15-year ONGC PEC; qualitative comparator. |\\n| PEER_K2 | [K2 Infragen business description](https://k2infra.com/) (Website, accessed for cutoff research) | EPC/turnkey,power/project engineering; qualitative smaller-EPC comparator |\\n\\nThe evidence ledger distinguishes verified disclosures (V), calculations (C), management claims (M) and analyst assumptions (A). evidence_ledger.json maps every material analytical topic to its source and limits. Original PDF/OCR files, account identifiers, transport helpers and private storage links are excluded from the source-safe deliverables. Source references use public issuer/exchange/rating links. Public filings' exact figures are preserved as factual inputs; long reproduced passages are not included.\\n\\nOpen issues affecting scoreability: independent audit closure, present available cash and credit, other-advance counterparties, bill/retention terms, quantified current customer concentration, backlog roll-forward and cancellation/escalation/liquidated-damage terms, project-level capital returns, actual weighted-average EPS reconciliation, restricted-cash vintage changes and semiconductor definitive financing. A source gap is ND/NS, not an invented neutral score.\\n\\nCalculation definitions and implementation: operating EBITDA=PBT+finance+D&A−other income; EBIT=EBITDA−D&A; NOPAT=EBIT×75% for positive EBIT (no artificial tax benefit on losses); financing IC=equity including paid warrant funds+interest-bearing debt−reported cash, with operating reconciliation separately shown; total-cash-capex FCF=CFO−cash operating capex; interest remains separate where classified in financing. Sales CAGR uses actual year intervals. All equations, input vintages, full event ledgers, factor contributions, reverse candidates and tests can be regenerated using standard-library Python only.\\n\\nUpdate log: initial v3.0 deep-research record for the 20 October cutoff. Material negative audit and cash findings override the apparent low P/E. Retrieval after the cutoff is restricted to documents dated by the cutoff. No unpublished H1 FY27 result, later market quote or hindsight outcome is used.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"original_heading\"","\"## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\"","5507","\"## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\"","5545","12214","\"## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\"","12238","14166","\"## 4. Business Overview\\n\"","14197","17135","\"## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\"","17164","19638","\"## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\"","19681","22551","\"## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\"","22592","27585","\"## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\"","27611","34579","\"## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\"","34618","44066","\"## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\"","44105","45714","\"## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\"","45742","46582","\"## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\"","46605","49204","\"## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\"","49253","50288","\"## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\"","50333","56486","\"## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\"","\"filename\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"historical_inputs.json\"","\"52e19146778c12604f0004eb00d1ab5bb99bb381dc539508c63afd7396e361ee\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"0796240cf68175aa311db74cf7cd9aa77c594a9ac5f532c4aeaf6aabc92e8905\"","\"Authored analytical table or source register, not a bundled original filing. Source-access qualifications remain in the research.\"","\"content\"","\"model_inputs.json\"","\"2f3a4fe711349e29ccc1c14b8d3affa6ad0bbc8d2e7cf8efdd79a86d5632be6f\"","\"3684125fc0496c548e0f7dfe587578957ae30469a24310e4ee7b3634979cb8ce\"","\"factor_evidence.json\"","\"c7d562fbb86ae0adca8576fa13512153c9e6ee34e42ea03f78728353e904d287\"","\"b4abe948fe832a5a6e9008811cfc38dfeaf529b63b9c2e935a84df21d99017a1\"","\"market_extract.json\"","\"019b741c16a4a38e161db0cf922ed816b4147f59cbd9277e8602092a2f1456e6\"","\"990e0f05b450b1fc7660427a0004714224ad2484d9a55a12a934a33691f942f0\"","\"source_register.json\"","\"824e3f98deb66d7253dfb2c0db352e86cf2b62a9ad6d2db09a68aa3379825384\"","\"567ba2406b6b5d4c8e4485d0f7f99168b8c241a6970dc65430fdba592c5d2c38\"","\"security_extract.json\"","\"7208906000c8ff22581d564839b42e001d1f4fa6f63127dd34ab2cc10444b20a\"","\"845db6e0ce